/* Reviewed comprehensive Lesson 6 teaching and original learning activities. */
STUDY_GUIDES["lesson-6"] = {"title": "Toddler and Preschool Nutrition", "subtitle": "Connect development, feeding relationships and nutrient needs with all 25 test topics and safe practice.", "source": "Comprehensive teaching aligned to the supplied Lesson 6 test and linked authoritative guidance, checked October 2026. Full textbook chapter and official marking key are not supplied. Historical, ambiguous and unsafe-to-generalize test assumptions are identified. Original practice is separate from the unchanged, unscored source test. Educational content, not individualized pediatric diagnosis or treatment.", "comprehensive": true, "sections": [{"id": "l6-1", "title": "1. Your learning route: nourish a growing person, not a perfect menu", "html": "<p>The toddler and preschool years combine slower physical growth with enormous changes in movement, language, independence and relationships. A child who ate enthusiastically as an infant may now inspect every green speck, refuse a familiar meal or request the same breakfast repeatedly. The counselling task is to understand what this means before trying to change it. Is the pattern compatible with normal development, is the food environment making eating harder, or is a medical or feeding problem interfering with intake?</p>\n<p>This lesson teaches the concepts behind all 25 questions in the supplied Lesson 6 test. It is not an official answer key. The complete assigned textbook chapter and instructor's marking key have not been provided. Several questions use historical terminology, broad disability generalizations or a numerical rule without naming its source. Those issues are explained openly. Do not turn a likely classroom convention into a clinical diagnosis, a restrictive diet or a promise to a family.</p>\n<p>Use four passes. First, learn how growth and autonomy change appetite. Second, connect nutrient needs to practical meals and snacks. Third, practise assessment: calculate carefully, interpret cautiously and ask what information is missing. Fourth, apply the concepts to feeding difficulties and medical conditions while recognizing when the child's health team must lead. You should be able to explain the reason for an action, not simply name a nutrient or select an option.</p>\n<p>Throughout the lesson, distinguish three levels of knowledge. A numerical fact is a reference value or calculation. An interpretation connects that fact with age, growth history, symptoms and context. An intervention requires an appropriate assessment and professional scope. For example, calculating BMI is not diagnosing a weight problem; recognizing low iron intake is not diagnosing anemia; noticing limited foods is not diagnosing autism or a feeding disorder.</p>\n<p>Your practical goal is a safe, adequate, workable feeding pattern and a less stressful relationship with food. A plan that looks impressive on paper but removes the child's few accepted foods, exceeds the family's budget or ignores swallowing safety is not a successful plan. The cases below are teaching examples, not individualized prescriptions.</p>"}, {"id": "l6-2", "title": "2. Growth, appetite and developmental readiness", "html": "<p>Infant growth is rapid; growth generally slows during the toddler and preschool years. This helps explain why a child's appetite may seem smaller even while they are more active. Intake also varies between days. A small lunch after a substantial breakfast does not prove a deficiency. Ask about the pattern across days, energy, symptoms and growth follow-up rather than evaluating a single plate.</p>\n<p>The supplied test states a toddler average of 8 ounces per month and asks for a preschool annual weight gain. A commonly taught approximate preschool gain is about 2 kg, or 4.4 lb, per year. Treat these as rough educational averages, not a minimum a child must gain each month. The 8-ounce statement itself equals half a pound monthly, or about 6 lb yearly, if mechanically multiplied. That arithmetic does not mean growth occurs evenly or that a child must be weighed monthly at home.</p>\n<p>Growth is a trajectory. Measurements need correct technique and an appropriate growth chart, with interpretation by the child's clinician. Consider past measurements, birth history, illness and family patterns. A single percentile is a position relative to a reference, not a grade. Crossing a centile line may require assessment, but the meaning depends on the full pattern. Measurements can also be wrong: shoes, different scales, a slouched height or a transcription error can materially change interpretation.</p>\n<p>Development changes how food is encountered. A toddler may want control, enjoy self-feeding, have limited patience for sitting and struggle to communicate discomfort. A preschooler can participate in simple food preparation and express preferences but still needs safe textures and supervision. Match the task to the child's actual abilities. Being a certain age does not automatically mean that hard raw foods or complex mixed textures are manageable.</p>\n<p>Teaching example: a parent reports, “She used to finish everything, and now she eats only half.” Before suggesting a supplement, ask what “everything” means, whether serving sizes increased, what drinks and snacks occur before meals, and whether growth and development are being followed. Also ask about pain, constipation, fatigue and changes in chewing. Normal developmental appetite variation and a concerning symptom can coexist; reassurance should not erase the symptom.</p>\n<p>Memory aid: <strong>PACE</strong> means Pattern, Appetite, Context and Evaluation. Look at the pattern over time, respect appetite signals, understand the context and seek evaluation when the history raises concern.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://caringforkids.cps.ca/handouts/healthy-living/when_your_child_is_a_picky_eater\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Canadian Paediatric Society: picky eating</a>.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/002013.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MedlinePlus: preschool development</a>.</p>"}, {"id": "l6-3", "title": "3. Responsive feeding, portions and predictable structure", "html": "<p>Responsive feeding combines reliable adult structure with attention to the child's signals. The caregiver plans what foods are available, when eating opportunities occur and where eating happens. The child has a role in deciding whether and how much to eat from what is offered. This is not the same as allowing unrestricted grazing, nor is it a reason to ignore a child who cannot meet needs without specialized support.</p>\n<p>A practical starting routine is three meals and two or three snacks, adjusted to the family's schedule and the child's needs. Include water access and avoid a constant stream of energy-containing drinks. Predictable opportunities reduce the need to negotiate about food all day. Include at least one familiar, accepted item alongside other foods when possible; the child does not need an entirely separate restaurant-style menu every time.</p>\n<p>The test's one-tablespoon-per-year-of-age idea is a <strong>starting serving heuristic</strong>. For a two-year-old, it suggests beginning with roughly two tablespoons of an offered food. It is not a total meal allowance, a rule for every food, a required amount to finish or a limit on seconds. Some foods fit this measurement poorly. A caregiver can simply start small, offer more when requested and avoid overwhelming the child with an adult-sized plate.</p>\n<p>Imagine a three-year-old offered a small amount of rice, soft beans, cooked carrot and a familiar fruit. The child eats the rice and fruit and asks for more rice. A responsive approach can provide more without demanding carrot as payment. Over the week, the caregiver continues offering varied foods. The goal is repeated opportunity, not extracting a bite through bargaining. If the child's accepted foods are narrowing markedly or growth is concerning, this routine must be paired with assessment rather than used as the sole intervention.</p>\n<p>Avoid measuring success only by an empty plate. Better observations include comfortable sitting, less conflict, a reliable snack schedule, willingness to tolerate a new food nearby and continued growth monitoring. Do not praise the amount eaten as moral achievement. Food is nourishment and enjoyment, not evidence that a child is “good.”</p>\n<p>For your practice, ask the caregiver to describe a usual meal from beginning to end. Who chooses the food? How long does it last? What happens after refusal? Does another caregiver use a different approach? Understanding the sequence often reveals a realistic first change without blaming anyone.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://peelregion.ca/children-parenting/toddlers-preschoolers/feeding-toddlers-preschoolers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Peel Public Health: toddler and preschool feeding</a>.</p>"}, {"id": "l6-4", "title": "4. Food learning: exposure, restriction and pressure", "html": "<p>Accepting a food is learned through repeated, safe experiences. Looking, smelling, touching and tasting can be different stages. A child who does not swallow a vegetable on the first offer has not failed. Re-offer foods without pressure, in manageable amounts and in forms appropriate to the child's skills. Modelling enjoyment is different from performing exaggerated approval or demanding imitation.</p>\n<p>The test asks how many exposures may be required and includes “more than 15.” There is no universal number after which every child accepts a food. Sources and studies use different foods, age groups and definitions of acceptance. The useful principle is persistence without coercion. Do not promise acceptance after eight tries or abandon a food because a particular count has been reached. A child's pain or sensory distress is not something to overpower with repeated exposure.</p>\n<p>Restriction and structure are not synonyms. Structure means the adult decides when and how foods are offered within a varied pattern. Highly controlling restriction makes a desired food forbidden or morally charged, or limits access in ways that increase preoccupation. Research on parental control suggests that restriction can increase interest in restricted foods and eating when access becomes available, though effects vary and family influences work in both directions. This does not mean every family boundary causes overeating.</p>\n<p>Consider two messages. “We will have cookies with our afternoon snack” provides a predictable boundary. “Cookies are bad, and you cannot have one until you eat vegetables” turns one food into a prize and another into a barrier. The second message may teach the opposite of the caregiver's intention. A counsellor can help redesign the routine without judging the caregiver who was trying to help.</p>\n<p>For the test, do not assume all options are correct just because a statement sounds negative. Increased desirability and overeating of a restricted food are distinct from disinterest in new foods. Read exactly what each option claims. For practice, describe the observed behaviour instead of attaching a label: “He asks repeatedly about the food that is kept off limits” is more useful than “He has no self-control.”</p>\n<p>An achievable experiment might be a small, neutral offering of one food twice during the week, beside a familiar food, with no demand to taste. Record comfort and curiosity rather than counting bites. If the child becomes very distressed, pause and review the approach with an appropriate feeding professional.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/infant-toddler-nutrition/foods-and-drinks/picky-eaters.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CDC: repeated food opportunities</a>.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10357749/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Fisher and Birch: experimental study of food restriction</a>.</p>"}, {"id": "l6-5", "title": "5. Energy and macronutrients: percentages are not portions", "html": "<p>Energy supports maintenance, movement and growth. Requirements vary with body size, activity, growth and health. A generic calorie chart is not a prescription for every child of the same age. Do not use an estimated requirement to override hunger signals or to start weight-loss restriction in a young child. When growth or medical complexity is a concern, a pediatric dietitian and clinician should individualize the plan.</p>\n<p>Know the fat AMDR distinction tested here: <strong>30–40% of energy for ages 1–3; 25–35% for ages 4–18</strong>. These are ranges for a dietary pattern, not a demand for every meal to match a percentage. A toddler should not automatically receive an adult low-fat diet. Fat contributes concentrated energy, essential fatty acids and absorption of fat-soluble vitamins. The kind of food and overall adequacy also matter; a percentage alone cannot describe diet quality.</p>\n<p>Worked example: suppose a hypothetical teaching menu provides 1,200 kcal. At 30% fat, 1,200 × 0.30 = 360 kcal from fat. Divide by 9 kcal/g to obtain 40 g of fat. At 40%, 1,200 × 0.40 = 480 kcal, or about 53 g. This calculation illustrates the range; it does not establish that a particular toddler needs 1,200 kcal or 40–53 g daily. Never confuse percent energy with percent of plate volume.</p>\n<p>For a hypothetical four-year-old menu supplying the same 1,200 kcal, 25–35% gives 300–420 kcal from fat, or approximately 33–47 g. The age band changes the range, not the conversion factor. Fat provides about 9 kcal/g, while protein and carbohydrate provide about 4 kcal/g. Check your arithmetic units: kcal multiplied by a fraction is still kcal; dividing by kcal per gram produces grams.</p>\n<p>Protein supplies amino acids for tissues and many functional molecules. Carbohydrate foods can provide energy, fibre and micronutrients. Practical meals combine these roles rather than isolating nutrients into separate “jobs.” For example, soft lentils provide protein, iron, carbohydrate and fibre; yogurt can provide protein and calcium; a fortified food's contribution depends on its label and amount eaten.</p>\n<p>Memory aid: <strong>Age → Range → Energy → Grams</strong>. Identify the age band before selecting the range, use an illustrative or clinically established energy value, then convert with the appropriate energy-per-gram factor. Numbers without those steps are easy to misuse.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/food-nutrition/healthy-eating/dietary-reference-intakes/tables/reference-values-macronutrients.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Health Canada: macronutrient reference values</a>.</p>"}, {"id": "l6-6", "title": "6. Iron, vitamin D and calcium: distinguish intake from treatment", "html": "<p>Three useful reference pairs are iron, calcium and vitamin D. Iron RDA is <strong>7 mg/day at ages 1–3 and 10 mg/day at ages 4–8</strong>. Calcium RDA is <strong>700 mg/day at ages 1–3 and 1,000 mg/day at ages 4–8</strong>. Vitamin D RDA is <strong>15 micrograms, or 600 IU, daily from age one</strong>. These are total-intake reference values for generally healthy children, not automatic supplement doses or treatment plans.</p>\n<p>Iron is needed for hemoglobin and other functions. A child can have iron depletion before obvious anemia symptoms. Diet history may identify risk, such as few iron-containing foods or large milk intakes displacing meals, but it cannot confirm blood status. Encourage appropriate iron sources such as meat, legumes, tofu or fortified cereals. Pairing plant iron with a vitamin-C-containing food can help absorption. Testing, diagnosis and therapeutic dosing belong with the child's qualified health professional.</p>\n<p>Vitamin D supports calcium regulation and bone mineralization. Do not equate the RDA of 600 IU total intake with an instruction to give every child a 600-IU supplement on top of all sources. Review age, food fortification, existing supplements and local clinical advice. Label concentrations differ. A dropper, a drop and a millilitre are not interchangeable units. An adult high-dose product can be inappropriate even when its ingredient name is familiar.</p>\n<p>Calcium-rich foods may include milk, yogurt, cheese, calcium-set tofu and suitably fortified alternatives, depending on age and the overall plan. Calcium content does not establish nutritional equivalence between beverages. Protein, fat, energy and other nutrients differ. A beverage labelled “fortified” can still be a poor main milk replacement for a young toddler.</p>\n<p>The test emphasizes iron and vitamin D as common nutrients of concern. Treat that as a priority for assessment, not a claim that every other nutrient is always sufficient. A child's diet, access to food, medical condition and exclusions can create very different gaps. A large body size does not rule out micronutrient inadequacy, and a small body size does not prove it.</p>\n<p>In a counselling record, write “few reliable iron sources reported; recommend pediatric dietitian/medical review” rather than “iron deficient” unless the diagnosis is established. The same discipline applies to supplements: document the actual product, concentration and use rather than guessing from “a children's vitamin.”</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/food-nutrition/healthy-eating/dietary-reference-intakes/tables/reference-values-elements.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Health Canada: mineral reference values</a>.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/food-nutrition/healthy-eating/dietary-reference-intakes/tables/reference-values-vitamins.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Health Canada: vitamin reference values</a>.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://cps.ca/en/documents/position/iron-requirements\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Canadian Paediatric Society: iron requirements</a>.</p>"}, {"id": "l6-7", "title": "7. Fibre, fluid and bowel comfort", "html": "<p>The current North American total-fibre Adequate Intake is <strong>19 g/day at ages 1–3 and 25 g/day at ages 4–8</strong>. The supplied test's five-year-old question also offers 10 g, which corresponds to the historical “age plus five” rule. Five plus five equals ten; that calculation does not make it the same recommendation as the DRI AI. If the instructor expects a legacy textbook convention, clarify it. In your notes, keep the framework beside the number.</p>\n<p>An AI is a reference estimate used when evidence is insufficient to set an RDA. It is not a precise threshold below which every child is deficient. Translate the concept into varied foods: fruit, vegetables, legumes and grains in developmentally suitable forms. A sudden push toward large amounts of bran can crowd out energy, worsen discomfort or make an already restricted diet harder to manage. Increase variety and fibre gradually while considering fluid intake and tolerance.</p>\n<p>Constipation is not simply “not enough fibre.” Pain, withholding, toileting experiences, medications and medical conditions can contribute. A child who anticipates pain may avoid passing stool, which can make the cycle worse. Ask about stool consistency, pain, frequency, blood, toileting posture and whether the child's clinician is involved. A supportive footrest and a calm routine can be part of care; punishment and pressure are not useful responses to accidents.</p>\n<p>Teaching example: a parent says the child eats fruit every day but still has painful stools. Do not answer, “Add more fruit,” and stop. Ask whether bowel movements are hard, whether stool is being withheld and whether there is abdominal pain or soiling. Persistent or significant symptoms need assessment. Severe pain, marked distension, vomiting, blood or a child who appears unwell require prompt clinical attention rather than a trial of a fibre supplement.</p>\n<p>Fluid needs include water from food and beverages. Do not treat total-water reference values as an additional amount of plain water to force. Offer water regularly and consider heat, activity, illness and medical restrictions. Juice is not required to meet hydration needs. In a child with loose stools, large quantities of juice may be part of the problem, not the solution.</p>\n<p>Memory aid: <strong>Fibre + Fluid + Function</strong>. What foods provide fibre? Is fluid available? Is the bowel pattern comfortable, and is the child able to use the toilet without fear or difficulty?</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://caringforkids.cps.ca/handouts/healthy-living/healthy_bowel_habits\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Canadian Paediatric Society: healthy bowel habits</a>.</p>"}, {"id": "l6-8", "title": "8. Work the BMI case, then stop before overinterpreting", "html": "<p>The test gives a five-year-old boy weighing 50 lb, approximately 22.7 kg, and measuring 45 inches, approximately 114 cm. Convert height to metres: 114 cm ÷ 100 = 1.14 m. BMI = weight in kilograms ÷ height in metres squared. Therefore 22.7 ÷ (1.14 × 1.14) = 22.7 ÷ 1.2996 = approximately <strong>17.47 kg/m²</strong>. Using unrounded imperial measurements gives about 17.36. The nearest offered answer is 17.4, with the small difference explained by rounding.</p>\n<p>Common error one: divide by 114 squared without converting centimetres to metres. Common error two: divide by height once instead of squaring it. Common error three: round height too early. Always show units and retain enough precision until the final result. If two valid methods produce slightly different last decimals, check the supplied rounding before assuming one method is wrong.</p>\n<p>Interpreting a child's BMI requires an age- and sex-specific reference. Adult cutoffs cannot be applied. Exact age in months matters, as does the selected chart system. In the CDC system, the 85th to below 95th percentile is termed overweight and the 95th percentile or above obesity. Older materials used “at risk of overweight” for the former category. Canadian care commonly uses WHO-based growth charts, whose age-specific interpretation must not be mixed casually with CDC terminology.</p>\n<p>The test's Question 9 does not supply the exact age in months or a chart. A value around 17.4 in a boy just turning five may fall in the CDC 85th-to-below-95th band, but do not invent an exact percentile or an official answer. The wording and age assumptions need clarification. Most importantly, a screening category is not a complete assessment of health, body composition, eating quality or family behaviour.</p>\n<p>In practice, speak privately and respectfully with caregivers. Review the growth trajectory, medical history, food security, activity, sleep and family concerns. Do not prescribe a restrictive diet based on this calculation alone or describe a child as a problem to be corrected. Use neutral language such as “Let's look at the growth pattern with your child's clinician.” A child deserves adequate nourishment and supportive care at every body size.</p>\n<p>Memory aid: <strong>Calculate, Chart, Context</strong>. Arithmetic produces the number; an appropriate chart helps position it; clinical context determines what, if anything, should happen next.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/child-teen-calculator/bmi-categories.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CDC: child and teen BMI categories</a>.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://cps.ca/en/tools-outils/who-growth-charts\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Canadian Paediatric Society: WHO growth charts</a>.</p>"}, {"id": "l6-9", "title": "9. Assess adequacy without judging a family", "html": "<p>A useful diet assessment includes what is eaten, what is offered but not eaten, drinks, meal timing, supplements, accepted textures and the practical conditions around food. Ask about a typical day and variation across several days. A record is a sample, not a verdict. A child may eat differently at daycare, with grandparents or during illness. Clarify quantities only to the precision needed for the question you are trying to answer.</p>\n<p>Look for missing roles rather than merely missing colours. Where are dependable energy sources, iron-containing foods, protein foods, calcium sources, vitamin D and sources of fibre? Which foods are actually accepted? What is the plan for a nutrient when a whole category is excluded? A beautifully varied menu that the child cannot chew is not nutritionally adequate in practice. Conversely, a visually simple meal can supply useful nutrients.</p>\n<p>Children with inadequate access to food, severe restriction, growth concerns, illness or feeding difficulties may need additional assessment. Vegetarian or dairy-free eating is not itself proof of deficiency; the adequacy of the replacement pattern matters. A weight-management program can also become nutritionally inadequate if it restricts a growing child's needs. Variety is generally protective but does not guarantee that every nutrient is sufficient.</p>\n<p>Separate an intake concern from a safeguarding concern. Food insecurity may reflect cost, transport, housing, storage or family disruption. Ask respectfully about access and connect the family to appropriate supports. Do not label poverty, cultural eating patterns or ordinary selective eating as neglect. Actual concerns about a child's safety need the appropriate professional and legal response, not an assumption drawn from a one-day menu.</p>\n<p>A practical assessment note might read: “Family reports consistent meals, several accepted protein foods and no chewing pain. Vegetables are currently limited; growth is followed by primary care. Agreed to offer one familiar vegetable form twice this week without pressure.” A different note might read: “Food repertoire declining, meals prolonged and coughing reported. Recommend prompt feeding/swallowing assessment; avoid unsupported texture changes.” The difference is the evidence and risk, not how compliant the child appears.</p>\n<p>The test's question asking who is not at risk is best understood as a comparison of described patterns. It should not teach that a broad group is inevitably deficient or that a varied diet makes screening unnecessary. Always leave room for the individual.</p>"}, {"id": "l6-10", "title": "10. Vegetarian and vegan patterns: replace functions, not just foods", "html": "<p>A well-planned vegetarian pattern can support childhood growth. The planning challenge changes with the foods excluded. A child who eats eggs and dairy has different available sources from a child avoiding all animal foods. Ask what the family actually eats rather than relying on a label. A vegan child requires a reliable vitamin B12 source through appropriate fortified foods and/or supplementation planned with qualified care.</p>\n<p>Consider energy density as well as nutrient density. Small stomach capacity and a bulky, very high-fibre pattern can make it hard for some children to eat enough energy. This is why automatically adding bran muffins to a meal already rich in fruit and vegetables is not a universal recommendation. Avocado, appropriate oils, soft legumes, tofu and safely prepared nut or seed products can have useful roles. Cheese is an option only when compatible with the family's diet and any allergy.</p>\n<p>Iron, zinc, calcium, vitamin D, B12, protein and omega-3 sources deserve deliberate review. “Plant-based” on a package does not tell you whether it meets these needs. Compare labels and accepted amounts. A child who drinks a low-protein beverage instead of milk may need an assessment of the whole replacement pattern, especially under age two. Do not assume that fortification alone solves differences in protein, energy and fat.</p>\n<p>Example: a caregiver wants to replace yogurt at snack time. Start by asking what role yogurt currently serves and which alternatives the child accepts. A suitable fortified soy yogurt may differ from a coconut-based dessert in protein and nutrient content. Another combination might use a different food plus a separately planned calcium source. The answer is not simply “choose any dairy-free product.”</p>\n<p>Maintain regular meals and snacks rather than expecting a toddler to consume large adult portions. Prepare legumes and vegetables in manageable textures. Use thinly spread or smoothly mixed nut butter when appropriate; whole nuts and thick spoonfuls introduce choking concerns. Avoid making a child earn access to energy-containing foods by first finishing large volumes of vegetables.</p>\n<p>For your practice, collaboration is a strength. A pediatric registered dietitian can assess a fully vegan or very restricted pattern in detail, including supplement needs and growth. Your role can include helping a family implement the agreed plan, read labels accurately and identify practical barriers, not declaring the diet safe or unsafe based solely on its name.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://caringforkids.cps.ca/handouts/healthy-living/vegetarian_diets_for_children_and_teens\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Canadian Paediatric Society: vegetarian diets</a>.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://odph.ca/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/FHNAG_2025_EN_Pediatric-Nutrition-Guidelines.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ontario Dietitians in Public Health: pediatric nutrition guidance</a>.</p>"}, {"id": "l6-11", "title": "11. Milk choices, choking safety and teeth", "html": "<p>Milk is one part of a diet, not a substitute for learning to eat family foods. Canadian guidance for non-breastfed young toddlers commonly uses about 500 mL daily of 3.25% milk, with no more than 750 mL, while iron-containing foods remain important. This does not mean a breastfeeding toddler must add that volume of cow's milk. A child's overall pattern and clinical circumstances matter. Large milk volumes can displace foods needed for variety and iron.</p>\n<p>Ordinary plant beverages are not recommended as the main milk source under age two. Some children need an appropriate commercial formula or individualized alternative plan. After two, fortified unsweetened soy beverage may be an option within an adequate diet, but other plant beverages vary substantially. Allergy, growth and the rest of the diet can alter the plan. Never dilute formula or modify its preparation to make a beverage “lighter.”</p>\n<p>Food safety depends on shape, texture, size and the child's skills. Supervise seated eating and avoid walking, running or playing with food in the mouth. Hard, round, sticky or difficult-to-chew foods can be hazardous. Modify foods such as grapes appropriately, soften hard vegetables and avoid whole nuts, popcorn and thick globs of nut butter for young children. A child with swallowing difficulty needs an individualized texture plan; a generic age-based list is not enough.</p>\n<p>Frequent exposure of teeth to sugary drinks or prolonged sipping can contribute to caries risk. Water between eating occasions and an appropriate dental routine are useful habits. Caregivers should receive age-appropriate advice from a dental professional about brushing and fluoride toothpaste. Nutrition counselling complements dental care; it does not replace examination of pain, cavities or oral-motor problems.</p>\n<p>Imagine a child declining firm foods because chewing hurts. Calling this “picky eating” misses a possible dental explanation. Ask whether the child avoids one side of the mouth, complains of pain or has had a dental assessment. Likewise, coughing or a wet-sounding voice after swallowing is not a behaviour problem to solve by insisting on more bites.</p>\n<p>For test preparation, remember that the “best food” is never judged by nutrients alone. A food can contain useful protein or iron and still be unsafe in the form offered. Nutrition, texture, allergy status and the child's actual feeding ability belong in the same decision.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://peelregion.ca/children-parenting/toddlers-preschoolers/feeding-toddlers-preschoolers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Peel Public Health: milk guidance</a>.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.albertahealthservices.ca/assets/info/nutrition/if-nfs-prevent-choking-on-food-in-children-under-four.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Alberta Health Services: choking prevention</a>.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://caringforkids.cps.ca/handouts/healthy-living/healthy_teeth_for_children\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Canadian Paediatric Society: dental health</a>.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/infant-care/infant-nutrition.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Health Canada: young toddler milk guidance</a>.</p>"}, {"id": "l6-12", "title": "12. The juice case: calculate volume and identify what is displaced", "html": "<p>The supplied three-year-old's record includes 8 oz of fortified juice at breakfast, 8 oz at lunch, 12 oz at snack and 12 oz at dinner. Total: <strong>40 fluid ounces</strong>. If interpreted as US fluid ounces, 40 × 29.57 is approximately 1,183 mL, or 1.18 litres. The test does not specify the ounce system; the main conclusion does not depend on the small conversion difference. This is a large juice volume for a young child.</p>\n<p>“Fortified” means specified nutrients have been added; it does not turn juice into a complete meal, make its fluid volume irrelevant or supply the same eating experience as whole fruit. A large beverage volume can reduce appetite for other foods. Juice carbohydrate that is not fully absorbed can also contribute to loose stools. Do not infer that every episode of diarrhea is caused by juice: infection, malabsorption and other conditions require consideration.</p>\n<p>The menu contains oatmeal, banana, macaroni with butter, crackers and white bread with butter. There are accepted foods here, which are useful starting points. The concerns are limited variety and heavy reliance on juice, not that bland flavours are inherently wrong. Do not shame the family or remove all accepted foods abruptly. Ask whether this is a usual pattern, whether the child has pain or chewing difficulty, and whether feeding has become more restricted over time.</p>\n<p>Potential next steps are hypotheses to explore, not automatic prescriptions. Adding an accepted milk-and-cheese sauce to macaroni may improve nutrient variety if dairy is tolerated and the texture is suitable. A soft pear or peach can extend fruit variety. Nut butter may be useful only if allergy status, texture and safe preparation are appropriate. A smoothie based on more fortified juice is not automatically the best solution when excessive juice is already central to the problem.</p>\n<p>For Question 23, therefore, an “all of the above” option needs caution. The textbook may intend easy-to-eat variety, but real-world safety and displacement still matter. A nutrition counsellor should not approve every listed food without knowing feeding skills and allergies. If a child relies on a narrow texture range, the feeding team may need to assess the reason before major changes.</p>\n<p>A useful practical experiment might be an agreed, gradual change in beverage timing or volume alongside one tolerated nutrient-containing food. Track comfort, acceptance and caregiver feasibility. Persistent diarrhea, dehydration, blood, significant pain or poor growth needs medical assessment rather than continued menu experimentation.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://medlineplus.gov/ency/patientinstructions/000693.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MedlinePlus: children with diarrhea</a>.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/digestive-diseases/diarrhea/treatment\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIDDK: diarrhea treatment and hydration</a>.</p>"}, {"id": "l6-13", "title": "13. Selective eating versus a feeding problem needing assessment", "html": "<p>Selective eating exists on a spectrum. Many children go through periods of limited preferences while still eating enough, learning skills and growing appropriately. Concern rises when intake is inadequate, foods or textures are progressively lost, distress is substantial, meals are unusually difficult or there are signs that eating is unsafe. The question is not whether the child likes vegetables; it is whether nutrition, skills, safety or participation are impaired.</p>\n<p>Ask about four domains: medical comfort, nutrition, feeding skills and the mealtime relationship. Does eating cause pain? Are there symptoms of reflux, constipation or allergy? Is the child able to chew and swallow the foods offered? Are meals organized in a way the child can manage? Does the family have reliable access to accepted foods? These questions help you understand the problem without assuming that refusal is deliberate opposition.</p>\n<p>Examples warranting referral include coughing or choking with food, recurrent chest illness associated with feeding concerns, persistent vomiting, a very restricted or shrinking food repertoire, faltering growth, dehydration and marked distress. Urgent symptoms such as breathing difficulty, severe lethargy or inability to maintain hydration need immediate medical attention. A nutrition lesson cannot replace an emergency plan or a clinical feeding evaluation.</p>\n<p>Depending on the issue, care may involve a physician, pediatric dietitian, speech-language pathologist, occupational therapist, psychologist or multidisciplinary feeding service. Their roles overlap but are not interchangeable. A swallowing assessment is different from a behavioural consultation, and nutrient analysis is different from diagnosing a gastrointestinal condition. A parent should not have to prove which specialty is correct before asking primary care for help.</p>\n<p>Document observations precisely. “Coughs with thin liquids according to parent” is more clinically useful than “bad eater.” “Accepts five foods and has lost three previously accepted foods in two months” is more useful than “picky.” Ask permission to share relevant information with the care team; do not distribute a child's personal history beyond the authorized purpose.</p>\n<p>Your supportive contribution may be small and valuable: reduce blame, organize a clear food-and-symptom history, help the family prepare questions, and reinforce the team's plan. Do not use pressure, withholding of safe foods or a rigid exposure schedule to manage a problem that may involve pain or impaired swallowing.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://uat-csd.albertahealthservices.ca/peas/Uploads/Click%20to%20download%20CPG.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Alberta Health Services: pediatric feeding disorder clinical guidance</a>.</p>"}, {"id": "l6-14", "title": "14. Disability, cerebral palsy and Down syndrome: individual needs", "html": "<p>Disability does not define a single nutritional requirement. A child may have altered movement, muscle mass, growth, chewing, swallowing, gastrointestinal function or access to food. Some need more assistance to eat; some have lower energy expenditure; others expend considerable energy moving or have additional medical needs. An assessment must identify which factors are present rather than applying one rule to every diagnosis.</p>\n<p>The test asks for an “up to” percentage of children with disabilities who have nutritional problems. Such estimates depend on which disabilities, ages, settings and definitions were studied. No universal prevalence is established by the question. Do not select or repeat a number as a current fact about all disabled children without the assigned source. The important learning point is that nutritional problems can be common in some groups and deserve proactive, individualized assessment.</p>\n<p>In cerebral palsy, mobility can materially affect energy expenditure. A child who walks with substantial effort may use more energy for movement than a child primarily using a wheelchair. However, a wheelchair does not by itself establish an exact calorie requirement. Muscle tone, body composition, growth, feeding efficiency and illness also matter. The test's directional comparison often points toward lower energy needs in the less mobile child, but that is not a formula for reducing intake independently.</p>\n<p>Children with Down syndrome can also have varied needs. Avoid assuming that all have the same appetite, activity level or body composition. The broad test principle is that an ongoing mismatch between energy intake and needs can affect health. Care should also consider conditions assessed by the child's medical team, such as thyroid or gastrointestinal problems, rather than attributing all growth differences to family feeding practices.</p>\n<p>A supportive plan should fit the child's participation and comfort. Positioning, equipment, timing, assistance and fatigue may matter as much as food selection. If a feeding plan is shared with school or daycare, clarity and consistency are important: who assists, what textures are prescribed, what signs require stopping, and whom to contact. A general nutrition counsellor should not invent a swallowing plan or change tube-feeding prescriptions.</p>\n<p>Remember <strong>Function before formula</strong>. Ask what the child can do comfortably and safely, what support is required, and how growth is being monitored. Then work with the qualified team on nutrition, rather than beginning with a diagnosis-based calorie multiplier.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1097/MPG.0000000000001646\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ESPGHAN: nutrition in children with neurological impairment</a>.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://cps.ca/en/documents/position/gastrostomy-tube-feeding\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Canadian Paediatric Society: family-centred feeding decisions</a>.</p>"}, {"id": "l6-15", "title": "15. Rett syndrome and autism: know the condition without stereotyping", "html": "<p>The test prints “Rem syndrome.” This appears likely to be a typographical or extraction error for <strong>Rett syndrome</strong>, given the option describing an X-chromosome genetic change with severe neurological effects. Most classic Rett syndrome is associated with pathogenic changes in MECP2 on the X chromosome. It is a neurodevelopmental condition, not a diagnosis made from a food preference. Feeding difficulty and growth concerns may be part of its clinical picture and require specialist care.</p>\n<p>For exam study, recognize the association between Rett and MECP2/X chromosome, while keeping the source ambiguity visible. Do not treat the misspelled word as a separate established syndrome. Also avoid simplifying genetics into a guarantee that every individual has the same symptoms or feeding needs. A nutritional plan depends on function and medical assessment, not merely the name of a condition.</p>\n<p>Autistic children may experience food selectivity, sensory differences, a preference for predictability or difficulties communicating discomfort. Preferences can involve texture, temperature, brand, presentation or routine. Some prefer crunchy foods, others smooth foods, and others mixed patterns. There is no universal preference for solids over liquids or one characteristic meal behaviour that establishes autism.</p>\n<p>Question 18 uses broad, dated wording, including descriptions of distress as “temper tantrums.” For practice, ask what the child is experiencing: pain, sensory overload, uncertainty, motor difficulty or communication frustration may be relevant. Do not assume manipulation or treat self-injury as a nutrition lesson to be corrected by withholding food. Significant distress or risk of injury requires appropriate clinical support.</p>\n<p>A practical conversation might begin, “Which foods feel safe and predictable to your child? What changes are hardest?” Preserve dependable nourishment while investigating whether nutrition is adequate. A feeding specialist can help with the pattern when needed. The goal is not to make a child appear less autistic; it is to support health, comfort, autonomy and participation.</p>\n<p>Be cautious with elimination diets promoted as treatment for autism. Do not remove gluten, dairy or other categories without a valid clinical reason and a replacement plan. A diagnosis of celiac disease or food allergy requires its own appropriate evaluation; autism alone is not that diagnosis. Families deserve evidence-based support rather than blame, promises of a cure or expensive unnecessary restrictions.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.nichd.nih.gov/health/topics/rett/conditioninfo/Pages/causes.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NICHD: causes of Rett syndrome</a>.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://cps.ca/en/documents/position/asd-post-diagnostic-management\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Canadian Paediatric Society: autism follow-up care</a>.</p>"}, {"id": "l6-16", "title": "16. Celiac disease: gluten avoidance after appropriate diagnosis", "html": "<p>Celiac disease is an immune-mediated condition in which gluten exposure damages the small intestine in susceptible individuals. It is not simply a preference for gluten-free food and is not the same as wheat allergy. The core grains avoided are <strong>wheat, barley and rye</strong>. Bulgur is a wheat product. Rice and corn are naturally gluten-free, although preparation and cross-contact can change whether a finished food is suitable.</p>\n<p>If celiac disease is suspected but not yet evaluated, do not recommend starting a gluten-free diet before the clinician's diagnostic plan. Removing gluten can affect test results and make diagnosis harder. Once diagnosed, a strict gluten-free diet is managed with appropriate clinical and dietetic support. Oats require particular care: use products labelled gluten-free and follow the child's specialist advice.</p>\n<p>The practical challenge is bigger than replacing bread. Review labels, shared toasters, preparation surfaces, school snacks and family cooking routines. Make sure the replacement pattern still supplies adequate energy, fibre and nutrients. Gluten-free products are not automatically nutritionally superior; some are lower in fibre or less fortified than the foods they replace. The child's accepted foods, culture and household budget still matter.</p>\n<p>Teaching example: a family removes every grain because they hear that gluten is a grain protein. That can unnecessarily reduce variety. Help distinguish the specific gluten-containing grains from naturally gluten-free options, while directing detailed cross-contact education to an experienced dietitian. Another family might switch only the bread but overlook a sauce or shared preparation surface; the issue is reliable avoidance, not how “clean” the diet appears.</p>\n<p>For your practice, support the clinician's diagnostic sequence and the established treatment plan. Do not diagnose celiac disease from bloating, constipation or selective eating alone. Document whether the condition is confirmed, who is overseeing it, and what education the family has received. A single symptom pattern can have several explanations.</p>\n<p>Memory aid: <strong>WBR</strong> means Wheat, Barley, Rye. Add the second reminder: <strong>Test before removing</strong> when diagnosis is still being investigated. These two steps prevent very different errors: missing gluten after diagnosis and obscuring diagnosis before testing.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/digestive-diseases/celiac-disease/treatment\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIDDK: celiac treatment</a>.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/digestive-diseases/celiac-disease/diagnosis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIDDK: diagnostic testing</a>.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/digestive-diseases/celiac-disease/eating-diet-nutrition\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIDDK: gluten-free eating</a>.</p>"}, {"id": "l6-17", "title": "17. Sickle-cell disease, transfusion iron and food-allergy breathing symptoms", "html": "<p>Repeated red-cell transfusions can introduce substantial iron into the body. This iron bypasses the usual regulation of intestinal absorption and can accumulate, so children receiving transfusion programs need specialist monitoring. Treatment may involve medically prescribed iron chelation. A child's anemia from sickle-cell disease does not automatically mean iron deficiency, and a counsellor should not recommend iron supplements simply because the word “anemia” is present.</p>\n<p>Question 21 asks which food may be inappropriate for a child with sickle-cell disease receiving a transfusion. Its likely teaching focus is iron exposure, with meat and vitamin C perhaps intended to draw attention to iron absorption. But the stem does not establish iron status, transfusion frequency or a prescribed restriction. <strong>A routine hamburger is not automatically contraindicated by one transfusion.</strong> Do not turn this item into a blanket ban on meat, fortified foods or vitamin-C-containing foods. The hematology team determines whether any dietary or supplement restriction is indicated.</p>\n<p>The distinction is important: dietary iron and transfusional iron are not identical clinical problems, and eliminating nutritious foods does not replace management of iron overload. Ask which supplements are being used and whether the treating team has provided instructions. Help implement that plan without adding unsupported restrictions. A medically complex child's energy, growth and hydration needs also require individualized care.</p>\n<p>Question 25 states that some children have asthma “as a result of” food allergies. This wording is too simple. Food allergy can trigger respiratory symptoms, including wheeze, during an allergic reaction; asthma and food allergy can coexist. Food allergy is not a usual explanation for all chronic asthma. Do not recommend broad food exclusions as routine asthma treatment.</p>\n<p>Breathing difficulty after eating may be anaphylaxis, especially with other allergic symptoms, and should not be treated merely as a dietary intolerance. Follow the child's prescribed emergency plan, use prescribed epinephrine when indicated and seek emergency help. Nutrition counselling is not a substitute for emergency treatment or an allergist's assessment. A food challenge should never be improvised during a counselling session.</p>\n<p>Exam skill: distinguish “can occur with” from “is generally caused by.” The former recognizes a possible association or trigger; the latter claims a much broader causal explanation. That wording discipline protects both test reasoning and clinical conversations.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/sites/default/files/publications/56-364NFULL.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NHLBI: sickle-cell disease and transfusional iron overload</a>.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.lung.ca/lung-diseases/asthma/managing-asthma/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Canadian Lung Association: asthma and food triggers</a>.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.foodallergycanada.org/food-allergy-basics/related-conditions/food-intolerances-and-other-conditions/asthma/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Food Allergy Canada: asthma and food allergy</a>.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.foodallergycanada.org/food-allergy-basics/preventing-and-treating-allergic-reactions/reaction-signs-and-symptoms/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Food Allergy Canada: allergic reaction signs and symptoms</a>.</p>"}, {"id": "l6-18", "title": "18. Memory anchors and legacy-question cautions", "html": "<p>Use these anchors to retrieve concepts, then explain them in your own words. <strong>PACE</strong>: Pattern, Appetite, Context, Evaluation. <strong>Calculate, Chart, Context</strong>: a BMI value is not a diagnosis. <strong>Age → Range → Energy → Grams</strong>: choose the correct AMDR before converting. <strong>Fibre + Fluid + Function</strong>: stool comfort is not solved by a fibre number alone. <strong>Function before formula</strong>: disability-related needs are individualized. <strong>WBR</strong>: wheat, barley and rye are the core gluten-containing grains in celiac disease.</p>\n<p>Keep a two-column study note for older test language. On the left, write the classroom convention exactly, such as “age plus five.” On the right, write the current reference and limitation, such as “DRI AI is 25 g for ages 4–8; not the same framework.” This prevents memorizing two numbers without understanding why they differ. Do the same for “at risk of overweight” versus current chart terminology and for the likely “Rem/Rett” typo.</p>\n<p>Do not memorize a disability prevalence percentage as though it applied to all children. Do not assume that an autistic child's food choices follow one stereotype. Do not turn a transfusion question into an iron-food prohibition. Do not approve all texture options without a safety check. These are not minor wording details: they change what a learner might tell a real family.</p>\n<p>The test asks which nutrition problem is least common among young children. Frequency depends on location, population and the definition of a problem. Lead exposure has declined substantially in Canada, but low-level exposure can still harm development and may be asymptomatic. A relative-frequency classroom question is not a reason to dismiss a child's actual environmental risk. Iron deficiency, caries and constipation are useful common concerns to recognize, not diagnoses to assign from a checklist.</p>\n<p>A strong short-answer explanation has three parts: the principle, the application and the boundary. For example: “Fat supplies concentrated energy; toddlers have a higher fat AMDR than older children; this does not justify prescribing a fixed fat intake without understanding the child's needs.” If you can add the boundary, you probably understand the concept more deeply than if you can only name the answer.</p>\n<p>For any ambiguous source item, retain the original wording, document the concern and check the instructor's expected framework. The source test remains unscored in the hub because no official answer key has been supplied. Your original practice below is designed to teach reasoning, not to imitate a hidden marking key.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/environmental-workplace-health/environmental-contaminants/lead.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Health Canada: lead exposure</a>.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://cps.ca/en/documents/position/lead-toxicity\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Canadian Paediatric Society: low-level lead exposure</a>.</p>"}, {"id": "l6-19", "title": "19. Your complete 25-question test-topic map", "html": "<p>This map identifies the concept behind every supplied test question. It is a study navigation tool, not an official marked answer sheet. Where wording is incomplete or historical, the limitation is part of what you should learn. Read the original question alongside the relevant teaching section and explain the reasoning before looking at options.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Topic</th><th>What to know and where to review</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Growth averages</td><td>Section 2: slower growth and variable appetite. About 2 kg/4.4 lb yearly is a rough preschool teaching average, not an individual target.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Starting portions</td><td>Section 3: one tablespoon per year of age is a starting heuristic, not a quota, whole-meal allowance or limit on seconds.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Restriction</td><td>Section 4: distinguish predictable structure from controlling restriction; assess individual claims rather than automatically choosing all of the above.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Repeated exposure</td><td>Section 4: repeated neutral opportunities may be needed; no universally guaranteed exposure count.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Fat AMDR</td><td>Section 5: 30–40% ages 1–3; 25–35% ages 4–18. Apply the range to energy, not plate volume.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Iron RDA</td><td>Section 6: ages 4–8, 10 mg/day. Intake reference is not a therapeutic supplement dose.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Fibre frameworks</td><td>Section 7: current AI 25 g/day for ages 4–8; historical age+5 gives 10 g for age five. Both appear among the options; clarify the intended framework.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>BMI calculation</td><td>Section 8: 22.7/1.14²≈17.47; exact imperial conversion≈17.36. Option 17.4 is closest; acknowledge rounded inputs.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Child BMI meaning</td><td>Section 8: use exact age, sex and named chart. The stem omits age in months/chart and mixes historical terminology; do not invent an exact percentile.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Common nutrient concerns</td><td>Section 6: iron and vitamin D deserve attention; other nutrients are not automatically adequate for every child.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Appetite changes</td><td>Section 2: appetite can decline as growth slows, but persistent symptoms or growth concerns still need assessment.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Deficiency risk</td><td>Section 9: varied intake is generally protective; restriction, illness, food access and replacement patterns matter. Diet label alone does not establish deficiency.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Relative frequency</td><td>Section 18: prevalence depends on population. Do not dismiss lead risk because it may be less common in a particular setting.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Vegetarian feeding</td><td>Section 10: review energy density, regular eating opportunities and B12. Extra bran is not a universal recommendation; cheese is not vegan.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Disability prevalence</td><td>Section 14: no universal “up to” percentage can be assigned without the textbook source and population definition.</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Down syndrome</td><td>Section 14: individualized energy balance and medical context; avoid assumptions that all children share one requirement.</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Likely Rett typo</td><td>Section 15: “Rem” appears to mean Rett; MECP2/X chromosome is the relevant association. Verify wording with instructor.</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Autism and eating</td><td>Section 15: sensory and routine differences vary; no universal solid/liquid preference or diagnostic food-choice profile.</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>Juice and diarrhea</td><td>Section 12: excessive juice can contribute to loose stools; investigate persistent or concerning symptoms rather than assuming the cause.</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>Celiac disease</td><td>Section 16: wheat, barley and rye; do not begin gluten avoidance before diagnostic testing without medical direction.</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td>Transfusion iron</td><td>Section 17: specialist monitoring, not a blanket hamburger or fortified-food ban. Stem lacks information for a safe clinical food prohibition.</td></tr><tr><td>22</td><td>Restricted menu</td><td>Sections 12–13: limited variety and large beverage volume are concerns; blandness alone is not pathology.</td></tr><tr><td>23</td><td>Expanding accepted foods</td><td>Section 12: consider texture, allergy and nutrient role; adding more fortified juice is not automatically appropriate.</td></tr><tr><td>24</td><td>Cerebral palsy energy</td><td>Section 14: mobility influences expenditure, but individual tone, composition, growth and health determine needs.</td></tr><tr><td>25</td><td>Food allergy and asthma</td><td>Section 17: food reactions can cause respiratory symptoms; do not equate this with food allergy usually causing chronic asthma.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>A useful study check is to cover the third column and explain each topic aloud. For calculation questions, write the units at every step. For clinical questions, state what the question tells you, what it does not tell you and what you would need before advising a real family. Being able to spot a missing assumption is a sign of understanding, not a failure to memorize.</p>"}, {"id": "l6-20", "title": "20. Original practice: reason first, then check", "html": "<p>These questions are original teaching practice, separate from the unchanged source test. Try explaining each answer before revealing or reading the explanation. When you miss an item, identify whether the gap was a fact, a calculation, a missing assumption or a scope boundary. That makes revision more useful than simply repeating a quiz.</p>\n<h3>1. A two-year-old eats a large breakfast and very little lunch. What should you assess before calling this inadequate intake?</h3><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Review the pattern across days, snacks and drinks, growth follow-up, energy and symptoms. One meal is not enough to judge adequacy. Ask whether the child is comfortably eating and whether portions have become larger than needed.</p>\n<h3>2. A caregiver serves two tablespoons of each food to a two-year-old and refuses seconds. What is the error?</h3><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> The tablespoon-per-year idea is a starting portion heuristic, not an upper limit. Offer more in response to appetite within a structured eating opportunity; do not turn the guide into a restriction.</p>\n<h3>3. Compare “Cookies are forbidden because they are bad” with “Cookies will be part of afternoon snack.”</h3><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> The second provides predictable structure without moralizing food. The first may increase preoccupation or make a food more desirable. This does not mean unlimited access is required; structure and coercive restriction are different.</p>\n<h3>4. A child has refused broccoli 12 times. Does that prove repeated exposure has failed?</h3><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> No fixed exposure count guarantees acceptance. Continue appropriate, low-pressure opportunities if comfortable, but investigate pain, distress or restricted feeding rather than simply insisting on more attempts.</p>\n<h3>5. For an illustrative 1,200-kcal toddler menu, calculate 30–40% of energy as fat in grams.</h3><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> 1,200×0.30=360 kcal and 1,200×0.40=480 kcal. Divide each by 9: approximately 40–53 g. This teaches conversion; it is not a prescription that the toddler needs 1,200 kcal.</p>\n<h3>6. What changes in that same illustration once the child is four?</h3><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> The fat AMDR is 25–35%, giving 300–420 kcal or about 33–47 g at 1,200 kcal. The age band changes; the 9-kcal/g conversion does not.</p>\n<h3>7. Why is “600 IU vitamin D” not automatically an instruction to add a 600-IU supplement?</h3><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> It is the RDA for total intake from age one. Review food sources, existing supplements and clinical guidance before determining an individual supplement plan. Product concentration must also be checked.</p>\n<h3>8. A five-year-old is assigned both 10 g and 25 g of fibre in different notes. Reconcile them.</h3><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Ten grams comes from the historical age-plus-five heuristic; 25 g is the current DRI Adequate Intake for ages 4–8. They are different frameworks, not equivalent calculations. Ask which framework the test intends.</p>\n<h3>9. Calculate BMI from 22.7 kg and 1.14 m, and explain the test option 17.4.</h3><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> 22.7÷1.14²≈17.47 kg/m², which rounds to 17.5 at one decimal. Exact conversion of 50 lb and 45 in gives about 17.36, rounding to 17.4. Input rounding explains the difference; 17.4 is the closest supplied option.</p>\n<h3>10. Why can you not apply an adult BMI cutoff to this five-year-old?</h3><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Children require age- and sex-specific growth-chart interpretation. Exact age in months and the chart system matter. A BMI category alone is not a complete diagnosis or a reason for a restrictive diet.</p>\n<h3>11. A vegan toddler drinks a fortified low-protein plant beverage. What information is missing?</h3><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Age, volume, growth, overall energy and protein, calcium/vitamin D/B12 sources, accepted foods and any prescribed alternative plan. Fortification does not establish equivalence to an age-appropriate milk source, particularly under two.</p>\n<h3>12. What is the first response to coughing repeatedly with drinks?</h3><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Treat it as a possible feeding/swallowing concern and arrange appropriate clinical assessment. Do not solve it by pressuring intake or independently thickening liquids. Breathing difficulty or severe acute symptoms require emergency help.</p>\n<h3>13. Add the test juice volumes: 8+8+12+12 fluid ounces. Interpret the result.</h3><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> The total is 40 fluid ounces, approximately 1.18 litres using US fluid ounces, or 1.2 litres with a 30 mL classroom approximation. It is a large juice load that may displace other foods; the label “fortified” does not remove that concern.</p>\n<h3>14. Why is bland food not, by itself, evidence of a feeding disorder?</h3><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Flavour preference is not a diagnosis. Look at adequacy, variety, skills, symptoms, distress, growth and participation. Limited variety plus very large beverage intake is more informative than the absence of strong seasoning.</p>\n<h3>15. Would adding a fortified-juice smoothie automatically fix the case menu?</h3><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> No. More juice could maintain excessive beverage intake and displacement. Consider accepted nutrient-containing foods, safe textures and an individualized gradual plan, with referral if feeding skills or adequacy are concerning.</p>\n<h3>16. Does wheelchair use give an exact calorie requirement for a child with cerebral palsy?</h3><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> No. Mobility influences expenditure, but tone, muscle mass, growth, feeding efficiency and health also matter. The clinical team individualizes needs and monitors response; a diagnosis or equipment label is not a calorie prescription.</p>\n<h3>17. What should you remember about the test phrase “Rem syndrome”?</h3><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> It likely refers to Rett syndrome, given the X-chromosome/neurological option. Most classic Rett involves MECP2 changes. Keep the typo visible and verify the assigned wording; do not invent a separate syndrome.</p>\n<h3>18. A caregiver wants to remove gluten before celiac testing. What is the concern?</h3><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Gluten avoidance can change test results and complicate diagnosis. Advise discussing testing with the clinician before exclusion. After confirmed celiac disease, strict avoidance of wheat, barley and rye requires a complete, nutritionally adequate plan.</p>\n<h3>19. A child with sickle-cell disease receives transfusions. Should you ban iron-rich foods or recommend iron for anemia?</h3><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Neither action follows automatically. The hematology team assesses iron status and transfusional overload. Avoid unprescribed iron supplements; do not replace specialist monitoring or chelation with blanket food restrictions.</p>\n<h3>20. A child wheezes after eating and develops swelling. Is this a routine nutrition follow-up issue?</h3><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> No. This may be a severe allergic reaction. Follow the prescribed emergency plan, use prescribed epinephrine when indicated and seek emergency help. Do not treat it only as ordinary asthma or wait for dietary counselling.</p>"}, {"id": "l6-21", "title": "21. What this means for my practice", "html": "<strong>Your first job is to understand the child and the family before redesigning their diet.</strong> Begin with the caregiver's concern in their own words. Ask what eating is like, which foods are dependable, how the child communicates hunger or discomfort, and what the family has already tried. Do not assume that the goal is more vegetables, less weight or a “cleaner” menu. The family may need less conflict, a manageable routine or help getting a feeding assessment.\n<p>Use a small set of practical questions: “What happens during a usual meal?” “Which foods and textures are comfortable?” “What does your child drink between meals?” “Any pain, coughing, choking or vomiting?” “Who is following growth?” “Are there foods you avoid, and what replaces them?” “What is realistically available and affordable this week?” These questions reveal actionable information without interrogating or blaming a parent.</p>\n<p>Choose one target that fits the evidence. For an otherwise well child with a reasonable repertoire, this might be a predictable snack routine or a neutral new-food opportunity. For a family with a restrictive diet, it might be organizing a pediatric dietitian review of reliable nutrient sources. For a child with coughing, loss of foods or growth concerns, the priority may be referral rather than a menu change. A good plan is not always the plan with the most dietary advice.</p>\n<p>Make application concrete. Instead of “increase variety,” agree on an accepted macaroni dish with one tolerable addition, if safe and appropriate. Instead of “drink less juice,” review current volume and timing, then collaborate on a feasible change without compromising hydration or disrupting a medically necessary feeding plan. Instead of “eat more fibre,” ask about stool pain and toileting and coordinate care if constipation is persistent. Check what the caregiver feels confident doing.</p>\n<p>Keep your scope visible. You can teach general food principles, support habits and help families implement a qualified team's plan within your training. You should not diagnose anemia, celiac disease, autism, a swallowing disorder or iron overload; prescribe therapeutic supplement doses; design unsupervised elimination diets; or alter medical nutrition and tube-feeding plans. Scope also depends on your actual credentials, competence and applicable professional requirements, not on completing one lesson.</p>\n<p>Document the concern, relevant observations, agreed action, referral advice and follow-up question. Avoid labels such as “noncompliant child.” A better note describes what happened and what support is needed. Respect consent before sharing a child's information with school or another provider. At follow-up, ask whether the plan was workable and whether symptoms or circumstances changed; do not judge success only by weight or bites eaten.</p>\n<p>Your closing practice question is: <strong>What can I help this family do safely this week, and what needs someone else's expertise?</strong> If you can answer both halves, you are connecting nutrition knowledge with responsible, person-centred counselling.</p>"}]};
EXPANDED_LESSON_TOOLS["lesson-6"] = {"flashcards": [["Normal appetite change after infancy?", "Growth generally slows, so appetite may decrease or vary; assess the pattern and symptoms, not one meal."], ["Starting portion heuristic?", "About one tablespoon per year of age is a starting amount, not a quota or limit."], ["Responsive feeding versus grazing?", "Responsive feeding respects cues within reliable eating opportunities; it is not constant unstructured access."], ["What makes a useful new-food exposure?", "Safe, neutral and low pressure; there is no guaranteed acceptance count."], ["Restriction versus structure?", "Structure sets predictable opportunities; controlling restriction can make food forbidden and preoccupying."], ["Fat AMDR ages 1–3?", "30–40% of energy."], ["Fat AMDR ages 4–18?", "25–35% of energy."], ["Convert fat Calories to grams?", "Divide kcal from fat by 9 kcal/g."], ["Iron RDA ages 1–3 and 4–8?", "7 mg/day and 10 mg/day, respectively."], ["Calcium RDA ages 1–3 and 4–8?", "700 mg/day and 1,000 mg/day, respectively."], ["Vitamin D RDA from age one?", "15 micrograms or 600 IU total daily intake; not an automatic supplement dose."], ["Fibre AI ages 1–3 and 4–8?", "19 g/day and 25 g/day, respectively."], ["Historical age-plus-five at age five?", "10 g/day; a different framework from the DRI AI."], ["BMI formula?", "Weight in kg divided by height in m squared."], ["Three steps after a child BMI question?", "Calculate, Chart, Context."], ["Does a varied diet guarantee adequacy?", "No; it is generally protective, but intake, health and individual needs still matter."], ["Vegan nutrient that needs a reliable planned source?", "Vitamin B12 from appropriate fortified foods and/or supplementation; assess other nutrients too."], ["Does fortified mean nutritionally complete?", "No. Added nutrients do not establish adequate protein, energy, fat, fibre or an appropriate eating pattern."], ["Milk guidance for toddlers aged 12–24 months?", "Whole milk may fit an appropriate non-breastfed toddler pattern; ordinary plant beverages are not equivalent main milk replacements."], ["Large juice-volume concern?", "Displacement of other foods and possible contribution to loose stools; do not assume every diarrhea episode has this cause."], ["Feeding referral clues?", "Coughing/choking, pain, shrinking repertoire, inadequate intake, growth concerns or marked distress."], ["Disability energy rule?", "Function before formula: individualize for mobility, tone, growth, body composition and health."], ["Rett syndrome association?", "Most classic cases involve MECP2 on the X chromosome; the test likely misspells Rett as Rem."], ["Autism eating stereotype to avoid?", "There is no universal solids-versus-liquids preference or diagnostic food-choice pattern."], ["Celiac core grains?", "Wheat, barley and rye; maintain diagnostic accuracy before starting exclusion."], ["Transfusion iron issue?", "Specialist monitoring of iron burden; do not automatically ban normal foods or prescribe iron."], ["Food allergy and wheeze?", "Respiratory symptoms can occur during allergic reactions; possible anaphylaxis needs the emergency plan."], ["Best final counselling question?", "What safe, feasible action helps now, and what needs another professional’s expertise?"]], "practice": [["A two-year-old eats a large breakfast and very little lunch. What should you assess before calling this inadequate intake?", "Review the pattern across days, snacks and drinks, growth follow-up, energy and symptoms. One meal is not enough to judge adequacy. Ask whether the child is comfortably eating and whether portions have become larger than needed."], ["A caregiver serves two tablespoons of each food to a two-year-old and refuses seconds. What is the error?", "The tablespoon-per-year idea is a starting portion heuristic, not an upper limit. Offer more in response to appetite within a structured eating opportunity; do not turn the guide into a restriction."], ["Compare “Cookies are forbidden because they are bad” with “Cookies will be part of afternoon snack.”", "The second provides predictable structure without moralizing food. The first may increase preoccupation or make a food more desirable. This does not mean unlimited access is required; structure and coercive restriction are different."], ["A child has refused broccoli 12 times. Does that prove repeated exposure has failed?", "No fixed exposure count guarantees acceptance. Continue appropriate, low-pressure opportunities if comfortable, but investigate pain, distress or restricted feeding rather than simply insisting on more attempts."], ["For an illustrative 1,200-kcal toddler menu, calculate 30–40% of energy as fat in grams.", "1,200×0.30=360 kcal and 1,200×0.40=480 kcal. Divide each by 9: approximately 40–53 g. This teaches conversion; it is not a prescription that the toddler needs 1,200 kcal."], ["What changes in that same illustration once the child is four?", "The fat AMDR is 25–35%, giving 300–420 kcal or about 33–47 g at 1,200 kcal. The age band changes; the 9-kcal/g conversion does not."], ["Why is “600 IU vitamin D” not automatically an instruction to add a 600-IU supplement?", "It is the RDA for total intake from age one. Review food sources, existing supplements and clinical guidance before determining an individual supplement plan. Product concentration must also be checked."], ["A five-year-old is assigned both 10 g and 25 g of fibre in different notes. Reconcile them.", "Ten grams comes from the historical age-plus-five heuristic; 25 g is the current DRI Adequate Intake for ages 4–8. They are different frameworks, not equivalent calculations. Ask which framework the test intends."], ["Calculate BMI from 22.7 kg and 1.14 m, and explain the test option 17.4.", "22.7÷1.14²≈17.47 kg/m², which rounds to 17.5 at one decimal. Exact conversion of 50 lb and 45 in gives about 17.36, rounding to 17.4. Input rounding explains the difference; 17.4 is the closest supplied option."], ["Why can you not apply an adult BMI cutoff to this five-year-old?", "Children require age- and sex-specific growth-chart interpretation. Exact age in months and the chart system matter. A BMI category alone is not a complete diagnosis or a reason for a restrictive diet."], ["A vegan toddler drinks a fortified low-protein plant beverage. What information is missing?", "Age, volume, growth, overall energy and protein, calcium/vitamin D/B12 sources, accepted foods and any prescribed alternative plan. Fortification does not establish equivalence to an age-appropriate milk source, particularly under two."], ["What is the first response to coughing repeatedly with drinks?", "Treat it as a possible feeding/swallowing concern and arrange appropriate clinical assessment. Do not solve it by pressuring intake or independently thickening liquids. Breathing difficulty or severe acute symptoms require emergency help."], ["Add the test juice volumes: 8+8+12+12 fluid ounces. Interpret the result.", "The total is 40 fluid ounces, approximately 1.18 litres using US fluid ounces, or 1.2 litres with a 30 mL classroom approximation. It is a large juice load that may displace other foods; the label “fortified” does not remove that concern."], ["Why is bland food not, by itself, evidence of a feeding disorder?", "Flavour preference is not a diagnosis. Look at adequacy, variety, skills, symptoms, distress, growth and participation. Limited variety plus very large beverage intake is more informative than the absence of strong seasoning."], ["Would adding a fortified-juice smoothie automatically fix the case menu?", "No. More juice could maintain excessive beverage intake and displacement. Consider accepted nutrient-containing foods, safe textures and an individualized gradual plan, with referral if feeding skills or adequacy are concerning."], ["Does wheelchair use give an exact calorie requirement for a child with cerebral palsy?", "No. Mobility influences expenditure, but tone, muscle mass, growth, feeding efficiency and health also matter. The clinical team individualizes needs and monitors response; a diagnosis or equipment label is not a calorie prescription."], ["What should you remember about the test phrase “Rem syndrome”?", "It likely refers to Rett syndrome, given the X-chromosome/neurological option. Most classic Rett involves MECP2 changes. Keep the typo visible and verify the assigned wording; do not invent a separate syndrome."], ["A caregiver wants to remove gluten before celiac testing. What is the concern?", "Gluten avoidance can change test results and complicate diagnosis. Advise discussing testing with the clinician before exclusion. After confirmed celiac disease, strict avoidance of wheat, barley and rye requires a complete, nutritionally adequate plan."], ["A child with sickle-cell disease receives transfusions. Should you ban iron-rich foods or recommend iron for anemia?", "Neither action follows automatically. The hematology team assesses iron status and transfusional overload. Avoid unprescribed iron supplements; do not replace specialist monitoring or chelation with blanket food restrictions."], ["A child wheezes after eating and develops swelling. Is this a routine nutrition follow-up issue?", "No. This may be a severe allergic reaction. Follow the prescribed emergency plan, use prescribed epinephrine when indicated and seek emergency help. Do not treat it only as ordinary asthma or wait for dietary counselling."]]};
window.NC_JOURNEYS["lesson-6"] = {"title": "Toddler and Preschool Nutrition", "kicker": "Connect development, everyday feeding and clinical boundaries to respectful family support", "steps": [{"id": "l6-interactive-1", "title": "A smaller appetite is not automatically a problem", "referenceSection": "l6-2", "teach": ["After infancy, growth generally slows and appetite may become less predictable. A child can eat enthusiastically one day and little the next. That observation belongs beside growth over time, activity, illness, development and the overall food pattern, not beside a demand that every meal look the same.", "The course uses average growth numbers to teach the difference between stages. Averages are not a monthly target every child must hit. Your counselling task is to notice patterns, listen to the caregiver and distinguish ordinary variation from persistent deterioration. Weight loss, pain, swallowing problems or declining intake need clinical assessment rather than reassurance based only on age."], "type": "choice", "prompt": "A caregiver says their 2-year-old eats less than they did as a baby, but is energetic and has routine growth follow-up. What is the best opening?", "options": [{"label": "Explain that appetite can change, then ask about the usual pattern, growth follow-up and symptoms.", "feedback": "Correct. You normalize a possibility while still checking the context and safety.", "correct": true}, {"label": "Prescribe a high-calorie supplement immediately.", "feedback": "A smaller appetite alone does not establish a need for supplementation.", "correct": false}, {"label": "Promise there cannot be a problem because the child is a toddler.", "feedback": "Normal developmental variation does not rule out illness, feeding difficulty or growth concerns.", "correct": false}]}, {"id": "l6-interactive-2", "title": "Offer structure without taking over the child’s appetite", "referenceSection": "l6-3", "teach": ["Caregivers can organize what food is offered, where eating happens and regular opportunities for meals and snacks. The child can respond to hunger and fullness within those opportunities. Structure is different from pressure: a predictable snack is not the same as grazing constantly, and an invitation is not a command to clear the plate.", "Start with a small serving and allow more when wanted. The historical “one tablespoon per year of age” rule is a starting-portion heuristic, not a maximum intake, complete meal prescription or test of whether a child has eaten enough. For a 3-year-old, three tablespoons illustrates a small first offering of a suitable food, not a total daily allowance."], "type": "sort", "prompt": "Sort each action by what it supports.", "categories": ["Supportive structure", "Overrides appetite or adds pressure"], "items": [{"label": "Offer meals and planned snacks at reasonably predictable times", "category": 0, "feedback": "Predictable opportunities support eating without requiring all-day grazing."}, {"label": "Offer a modest portion with more available", "category": 0, "feedback": "Small first servings reduce the pressure of a large plate; a child may ask for more."}, {"label": "Require a clean plate before leaving the table", "category": 1, "feedback": "This substitutes an external rule for the child’s fullness signals."}, {"label": "Make dessert the reward for finishing vegetables", "category": 1, "feedback": "A food bargain can turn one food into a prize and another into an obstacle."}]}, {"id": "l6-interactive-7", "title": "Exposure is an opportunity, not a countdown", "referenceSection": "l6-4", "teach": ["An unfamiliar food may need many calm encounters. Seeing, smelling, touching and tasting can be different stages of learning. There is no exposure number that guarantees acceptance; age, sensory preferences, previous experience and the way the food is offered all matter.", "Use a familiar food alongside a small optional new food, model eating it and keep the setting low-pressure. Repeated refusal is not a reason to punish the child or declare the food permanently impossible. Highly restrictive rules and “forbidden food” messages can increase a food’s special status. Ordinary household structure is not equivalent to shaming, bargaining or using hunger to force compliance."], "type": "choice", "prompt": "After eight offers of cooked carrot, a child still declines it. What next step best fits responsive feeding?", "options": [{"label": "Require eight bites because eight exposures should have worked.", "feedback": "An exposure estimate is not a deadline and does not justify forcing bites.", "correct": false}, {"label": "Keep calm opportunities available, perhaps in another safe preparation, with familiar foods and no pressure.", "feedback": "Correct. You support learning without treating acceptance as a test the child must pass.", "correct": true}, {"label": "Hide all vegetables indefinitely and never discuss food again.", "feedback": "A short-term workaround does not replace developmentally appropriate opportunities to learn about foods.", "correct": false}]}, {"id": "l6-interactive-9", "title": "Calculate BMI, then pause before interpreting", "referenceSection": "l6-8", "teach": ["BMI divides weight in kilograms by height in metres squared. For the supplied case, 22.7 kg and 1.14 m give 22.7 ÷ (1.14 × 1.14), approximately 17.47 kg/m². The test’s rounded measurements and answer options explain why 17.4 is the closest offered number; it is not a separate biological threshold.", "Children are not interpreted using adult BMI cut-offs. Assessment needs age, sex, a specified growth reference, accurate measurements and the growth trajectory. “Five years” without exact age does not permit a precise percentile, and historical labels differ from current ones. A calculated value alone is not a diagnosis or permission to put a child on a restrictive diet."], "type": "number", "prompt": "Using 22.7 kg and 1.14 m, calculate BMI to one decimal place.", "answer": 17.5, "tolerance": 0.1, "unit": "kg/m²", "explanation": "1.14² = 1.2996; 22.7 ÷ 1.2996 = 17.47, or 17.5 to one decimal. The source test offers 17.4 as its closest answer. Interpretation still needs an appropriate child growth reference and exact age."}, {"id": "l6-interactive-10", "title": "Turn a fat percentage into grams", "referenceSection": "l6-5", "teach": ["The fat AMDR is 30–40% of energy for ages 1–3 and 25–35% for ages 4–18. Fat contributes energy, essential fatty acids and support for absorption of fat-soluble vitamins. Young children should not automatically be placed on an adult low-fat eating pattern.", "To convert a percentage into grams, multiply total energy by the decimal fraction and divide the resulting fat calories by 9 kcal/g. A classroom total such as 1,200 kcal is an arithmetic assumption, not a universal prescription for every child. A balanced pattern includes suitable sources such as oils, avocado and age-appropriate nut or seed preparations, while accounting for allergies and swallowing safety."], "type": "number", "prompt": "For an arithmetic example only, a 1,200-kcal pattern gets 30% of energy from fat. How many grams of fat is that?", "answer": 40, "tolerance": 0, "unit": "g", "explanation": "1,200 × 0.30 = 360 kcal from fat; 360 ÷ 9 = 40 g. This calculates the given example, not the individual energy requirement of a child."}, {"id": "l6-interactive-11", "title": "Keep a fibre shortcut separate from the DRI", "referenceSection": "l6-7", "teach": ["The older “age plus five grams” shortcut gives 10 g for a 5-year-old. Health Canada’s total-fibre Adequate Intake is 19 g/day for ages 1–3 and 25 g/day for ages 4–8. These are different reference systems, not interchangeable answers to an unspecified question.", "The source test’s wording does not identify its framework, and both 10 g and 25 g appear among its choices. Learn the distinction rather than memorizing an unsupported official key. In practice, build fibre through varied foods and adequate fluids; avoid abruptly adding large amounts of bran or supplements that crowd out energy or worsen symptoms. Persistent constipation needs assessment, not simply an ever-higher fibre target."], "type": "explore", "prompt": "Predict the answer under each framework, then open the card.", "panels": [{"label": "Historical shortcut for age 5", "text": "5 + 5 = 10 g/day. Label this as the historical shortcut, not the current DRI value."}, {"label": "Current DRI age group 4–8", "text": "The total-fibre AI is 25 g/day. An AI is a reference for planning, not a diagnosis of deficiency from one day below it."}, {"label": "The test asks only “general recommendation”", "text": "The intended framework is unclear. Keep both concepts in your notes and seek course clarification rather than claim an official answer."}, {"label": "The child has painful stools", "text": "Review intake, fluids, routine and symptoms, and arrange clinical care when needed. Pain, withholding and persistent problems are not solved by a number alone."}]}, {"id": "l6-interactive-4", "title": "A nutrient reference is not a supplement prescription", "referenceSection": "l6-6", "teach": ["Iron supports oxygen transport and development. The iron RDA is 7 mg/day for ages 1–3 and 10 mg/day for ages 4–8. These are total-intake references; treatment doses for diagnosed deficiency are a different matter. Consider what the child actually eats, including iron-rich foods and reliable fortified products.", "Iron and vitamin D deserve deliberate attention, but the source test’s population generalization cannot establish which nutrients a particular child lacks. Review milk and drink volumes, supplements, restricted foods and growth history. A family may need practical food ideas, a label review or a referral. Do not diagnose iron deficiency from tiredness or recommend medicinal iron just because a child has a small food repertoire."], "type": "choice", "prompt": "A parent asks whether their 5-year-old should take 10 mg iron because that is the RDA. Which reply is most accurate?", "options": [{"label": "Yes, every 5-year-old needs a 10 mg supplement.", "feedback": "The RDA includes food intake and is not an automatic supplement dose.", "correct": false}, {"label": "No child ever needs iron supplements.", "feedback": "Some children need clinician-directed supplementation or treatment.", "correct": false}, {"label": "The 10 mg value is a total-intake reference. Review food sources and discuss suspected deficiency or supplementation with the child’s clinician.", "feedback": "Correct. You distinguish routine food planning from clinical diagnosis and dosing.", "correct": true}]}, {"id": "l6-interactive-12", "title": "Plan a plant-based pattern, not just a protein total", "referenceSection": "l6-10", "teach": ["A vegetarian or vegan eating pattern needs deliberate planning for a young child’s small appetite and growth. Review energy density, iron, vitamin B12, calcium, vitamin D and other nutrients alongside protein. A dairy-free label alone does not establish deficiency, and a protein total alone does not establish adequacy.", "HealthLink BC advises reliable B12 sources and individualized advice when intake is uncertain. Some bulky, high-fibre foods can fill a small stomach before enough energy is eaten. Nut butters must be prepared safely, and drinks differ greatly in energy, protein and fortification. Plant beverages are not suitable main milk replacements under age two without individualized guidance; a paediatric dietitian can help when a milk-free pattern or multiple restrictions makes adequacy difficult."], "type": "sort", "prompt": "Which suggestions support careful planning rather than assumptions?", "categories": ["Useful planning step", "Unsupported shortcut"], "items": [{"label": "Check reliable vitamin B12 sources and the exact fortified-food labels", "category": 0, "feedback": "A nutrient source must be reliable and actually consumed, not merely present somewhere in the diet."}, {"label": "Add appropriate energy-dense foods in safe forms when needed", "category": 0, "feedback": "Small stomach capacity makes energy density relevant; choose foods that fit skills, allergies and family values."}, {"label": "Assume any almond drink is nutritionally interchangeable with milk", "category": 1, "feedback": "Products differ in protein, energy and fortification, and age changes suitability."}, {"label": "Tell every vegan toddler to fill up on bran at every meal", "category": 1, "feedback": "Very bulky intake can displace energy and other nutrients; more fibre is not always the missing piece."}]}, {"id": "l6-interactive-3", "title": "Add the drinks before changing the menu", "referenceSection": "l6-12", "teach": ["The supplied 3-year-old case lists juice at breakfast, lunch, snack and dinner: 8 + 8 + 12 + 12 fluid ounces. That totals 40 fluid ounces, approximately 1.18 litres if US fluid ounces are intended. Fortification does not remove the volume, sugar exposure or potential displacement of other foods.", "An incomplete food record cannot diagnose a feeding disorder, but this pattern deserves questions. Confirm the usual amount, cup size, whether the day was typical, other drinks, appetite and stools. A caregiver may be relying on juice because they believe the fortification protects the child. Start by understanding that intention; then explain why a beverage cannot replace a varied food pattern."], "type": "number", "prompt": "Add the four juice servings: 8 + 8 + 12 + 12. What is the total?", "answer": 40, "tolerance": 0, "unit": "fl oz/day", "explanation": "The total is 40 fluid ounces. Using about 29.6 mL per US fluid ounce gives approximately 1,184 mL. The record is a clue to investigate, not proof of a particular diagnosis."}, {"id": "l6-interactive-13", "title": "Build from an accepted food without hiding the concern", "referenceSection": "l6-12", "teach": ["The case includes oatmeal, banana, buttered macaroni, crackers and white bread. The concerns are restricted variety and a very large juice volume, not the fact that foods taste bland. Sensory preferences are information that can help design a manageable next step.", "Keep an accepted food available while exploring a small, safe variation. For example, a child who accepts macaroni may be willing to explore a sauce separately before it is mixed in. Milk, cheese, a soft alternative or a spread must fit allergy history, nutrition needs and chewing ability. More fortified juice or a juice smoothie is not automatically the solution to a record already dominated by juice. Avoid removing every reliable food at once."], "type": "choice", "prompt": "Which first counselling plan best fits this record?", "options": [{"label": "Replace every accepted food with unfamiliar foods immediately.", "feedback": "That can remove the child’s dependable intake and increase distress without assessing feeding skills.", "correct": false}, {"label": "Explore the juice pattern and feeding concerns, preserve accepted foods, and agree on one safe food variation with appropriate referral if the range is very limited.", "feedback": "Correct. This addresses the pattern while protecting intake and trust.", "correct": true}, {"label": "Add a large fortified juice smoothie because fortification guarantees adequacy.", "feedback": "It may perpetuate displacement; fortification does not make the overall pattern complete.", "correct": false}]}, {"id": "l6-interactive-5", "title": "Nutrients do not cancel choking risk", "referenceSection": "l6-11", "teach": ["A food may contain useful nutrients but still be unsafe in its current form. Young children need a seated, supervised eating setting and foods prepared for their actual chewing and swallowing skills. Whole nuts, popcorn, hard round foods and thick spoonfuls of nut butter are important hazards.", "Change shape and texture deliberately: thinly spread smooth nut butter, prepare grapes in small lengthwise pieces, and soften or grate hard produce as appropriate. Never assume a generic food-list answer is safe for every child. Known swallowing difficulty requires an individualized feeding plan; do not independently thicken liquids or change prescribed textures. Caregiver first-aid training complements, but does not replace, prevention."], "type": "sort", "prompt": "Sort the preparation, assuming no food allergy and age-appropriate eating skills.", "categories": ["Safer preparation principle", "Choking concern"], "items": [{"label": "Smooth nut butter spread thinly on suitable bread", "category": 0, "feedback": "Thin spreading avoids a large sticky lump; the bread and portion still need to fit the child’s skills."}, {"label": "A spoonful of thick peanut butter", "category": 1, "feedback": "A sticky lump can obstruct the airway even though the food contains nutrients."}, {"label": "A whole grape for a young child", "category": 1, "feedback": "Round, firm foods need appropriate modification; supervision alone does not remove the shape hazard."}, {"label": "Soft cooked vegetable pieces appropriate to chewing ability", "category": 0, "feedback": "Texture and size should fit the individual child, who should eat seated and supervised."}]}, {"id": "l6-interactive-8", "title": "Know when “picky” is not enough of an explanation", "referenceSection": "l6-13", "teach": ["Food selectivity exists on a spectrum. A narrow repertoire can reflect sensory preferences, pain, constipation, oral-motor difficulty, anxiety or a combination. Investigate without labelling the child as manipulative. Persistent loss of foods, nutritional compromise or distress can require a multidisciplinary feeding assessment.", "Ask about coughing, choking, wet or altered voice during meals, long exhausting feeds, vomiting, pain and growth concerns. A nutrition counsellor can document observations, support feasible routines and coordinate a referral; they do not diagnose aspiration or a feeding disorder from a checklist. Acute inability to breathe, severe allergic symptoms or a child who is seriously unwell requires emergency care, not a routine food experiment."], "type": "choice", "prompt": "A preschooler coughs repeatedly with drinks, takes very long meals and has lost weight. What should take priority?", "options": [{"label": "Try a stronger reward for finishing meals.", "feedback": "This misses possible swallowing and medical problems and may increase pressure.", "correct": false}, {"label": "Arrange prompt medical and feeding/swallowing assessment, while following any existing safety plan.", "feedback": "Correct. These signs require assessment before routine expansion of foods or textures.", "correct": true}, {"label": "Independently thicken every drink to a consistency found online.", "feedback": "Texture prescriptions depend on swallowing assessment and should not be improvised.", "correct": false}]}, {"id": "l6-interactive-14", "title": "Do not let a diagnosis become an energy formula", "referenceSection": "l6-14", "teach": ["Children with cerebral palsy or Down syndrome may have feeding, growth and health needs that differ from those of peers. Mobility is one influence on energy expenditure, but muscle tone, movement effort, body composition, growth and medical status also matter. A wheelchair does not tell you the exact amount a child should eat.", "The source test compares a nonambulatory and ambulatory child with cerebral palsy as a broad teaching example. Real care must individualize and monitor. Both too little and too much energy relative to needs can cause problems. Review swallowing safety, meal duration, constipation, medication effects and the care team’s plan. Diagnosis-specific growth assessment is a clinical task, not a reason to aim every child at the same weight."], "type": "explore", "prompt": "What would you ask before changing food quantities?", "panels": [{"label": "The child uses a wheelchair", "text": "Ask about actual movement, muscle tone, growth trajectory, feeding assistance and the existing nutrition plan. Mobility alone does not establish an energy prescription."}, {"label": "The child walks but tires during meals", "text": "Ambulation does not rule out feeding difficulty. Meal effort, swallowing and intake still need assessment."}, {"label": "The family is worried about weight gain", "text": "Use respectful language, verify measurements and clinical follow-up, and avoid adult weight-loss dieting or blame."}, {"label": "A tube-feeding regimen is in place", "text": "Support the agreed care plan and communicate observations. Formula, volume and administration changes belong with the responsible clinical team."}]}, {"id": "l6-interactive-15", "title": "Describe sensory needs, not stereotypes", "referenceSection": "l6-15", "teach": ["Autistic children are individuals. Some have strong preferences for texture, brand, temperature, presentation or routine; others do not. Refusal and distress should prompt questions about communication, sensory experience, pain and skills. A general claim that autistic children prefer solids or liquids is not a safe clinical rule.", "The test appears to use “Rem syndrome” where Rett syndrome is intended. Rett syndrome is a neurodevelopmental condition commonly associated with a change in MECP2 on the X chromosome; feeding difficulties can be part of its care needs. That recognition fact does not permit diagnosis from food choices. Keep genetic conditions, autism and cerebral palsy distinct while recognizing that coordinated feeding support may help across them."], "type": "choice", "prompt": "An autistic child accepts one brand of cracker and becomes distressed when it is replaced. Which response is most helpful?", "options": [{"label": "Assume the child is choosing to be difficult and remove the accepted food.", "feedback": "This can threaten reliable intake and overlooks sensory, communication or medical explanations.", "correct": false}, {"label": "Ask what stays predictable about the accepted food, assess pain and nutritional concerns, and plan gradual support with the family and relevant team.", "feedback": "Correct. You work from the individual’s needs rather than a stereotype.", "correct": true}, {"label": "Diagnose Rett syndrome from the narrow food preference.", "feedback": "Food selectivity does not diagnose Rett syndrome or any other neurodevelopmental condition.", "correct": false}]}, {"id": "l6-interactive-16", "title": "Protect the diagnostic pathway before removing gluten", "referenceSection": "l6-16", "teach": ["Celiac disease is an immune-mediated condition triggered by gluten, not simply a preference for avoiding wheat. Confirmed disease requires avoidance of wheat, barley and rye, attention to cross-contact and appropriate follow-up. Naturally gluten-free foods can contribute variety, but labels and preparation still matter.", "Starting a gluten-free diet before evaluation can make diagnostic testing less reliable. A child with symptoms suggesting celiac disease needs medical assessment rather than an experimental broad restriction directed by a trainee. After diagnosis, a paediatric dietitian can support adequate energy, fibre and micronutrients as well as practical gluten-free food choices. Symptoms alone cannot distinguish celiac disease from other causes."], "type": "choice", "prompt": "A parent wants to remove gluten immediately because their child has recurring abdominal symptoms and has not been tested. What is the safest guidance?", "options": [{"label": "Arrange medical evaluation and discuss testing before starting a gluten-free diet.", "feedback": "Correct. Dietary changes can affect test results, so preserve the diagnostic pathway.", "correct": true}, {"label": "Remove wheat, rye and barley now and use symptom improvement as a definite diagnosis.", "feedback": "Symptoms and response to restriction do not establish celiac disease, and testing may be affected.", "correct": false}, {"label": "Remove rice and corn as well because all grains contain gluten.", "feedback": "Rice and corn are naturally gluten-free; unnecessary exclusions reduce options.", "correct": false}]}, {"id": "l6-interactive-17", "title": "Transfusion-related iron is not a food-ranking exercise", "referenceSection": "l6-17", "teach": ["Sickle-cell disease and iron-deficiency anemia are different conditions. Repeated transfusions can lead to iron overload, which is assessed and treated by the hematology team. Do not infer iron deficiency from the word anemia or recommend iron supplements without the treating team’s direction.", "The test’s single-food question oversimplifies this issue. It is not a sound basis for banning all iron-containing foods or deciding that low-nutrient snack foods are preferable to balanced meals. Review the written care plan, actual laboratory assessment and clinician advice. Food counselling can support adequate nutrition and practical routines without replacing transfusion monitoring or chelation treatment."], "type": "choice", "prompt": "A child with sickle-cell disease receives regular transfusions. A caregiver asks whether to start iron because the child has anemia. What is the appropriate response?", "options": [{"label": "Start iron because anemia always means iron deficiency.", "feedback": "Sickle-cell disease does not automatically indicate iron deficiency, and transfusions can add iron.", "correct": false}, {"label": "Ask the hematology team before starting iron and follow the child’s individual nutrition plan.", "feedback": "Correct. Treatment and iron-status interpretation belong with the responsible clinical team.", "correct": true}, {"label": "Remove every iron-containing food and replace meals with chips.", "feedback": "That is neither a balanced diet nor a treatment for transfusion-related iron overload.", "correct": false}]}, {"id": "l6-interactive-18", "title": "Separate allergy emergencies from routine asthma counselling", "referenceSection": "l6-17", "teach": ["An allergic reaction to food can include breathing difficulty or wheeze, sometimes as part of anaphylaxis. Asthma and food allergy can coexist, but that does not mean all childhood asthma is caused by food or that removing common foods is a general asthma treatment.", "When breathing symptoms begin suddenly after food, the priority is the child’s emergency plan and urgent care, not a trial of another food or waiting for a nutrition appointment. For a known allergy, support the prescribed avoidance and emergency plan and coordinate with the clinician. Do not advise a broad elimination diet solely because a child has asthma; unnecessary restrictions can create nutritional and practical burdens."], "type": "choice", "prompt": "During a meal, a child develops sudden breathing difficulty and swelling. What takes priority?", "options": [{"label": "Wait to see whether avoiding the food tomorrow helps.", "feedback": "A possible severe allergic reaction requires immediate action, not observation until tomorrow.", "correct": false}, {"label": "Follow the prescribed emergency allergy plan and call emergency services.", "feedback": "Correct. Acute breathing difficulty and swelling are an emergency; dietary counselling comes later.", "correct": true}, {"label": "Offer more of the food to confirm which ingredient caused it.", "feedback": "Re-exposure can be dangerous and is not an appropriate diagnostic experiment.", "correct": false}]}, {"id": "l6-interactive-19", "title": "Turn the whole story into one feasible next step", "referenceSection": "l6-9", "teach": ["A food record is a starting point, not a verdict. Ask about several usual days, food access, culture, routines, accepted foods, drinks, supplements and symptoms. The same short list can mean different things when a family is managing sensory needs, food insecurity, a recent illness or an established clinical plan.", "Choose an action that addresses the most important concern without overwhelming the family. For a well child, that may be a planned snack with an iron-rich food or a modest change in the drink routine. For a child with growth, pain or swallowing concerns, assessment comes first. Document what you know, what is uncertain, the agreed step and how progress will be reviewed. A useful outcome is safer, more workable feeding, not a perfectly coloured plate."], "type": "explore", "prompt": "Build your consultation plan in this order.", "panels": [{"label": "1 · Establish safety", "text": "Check acute illness, hydration, swallowing, pain and growth concerns. Escalate when needed before experimenting with a new routine."}, {"label": "2 · Understand the pattern", "text": "Clarify accepted foods, usual drinks, meal opportunities, family resources and what the caregiver hopes will change."}, {"label": "3 · Agree on one realistic action", "text": "Choose a safe, specific step that preserves reliable intake and fits the household rather than handing over a rigid ideal menu."}, {"label": "4 · Review and coordinate", "text": "Decide what will be observed and when to revisit it. Coordinate with the child’s clinical or feeding team for issues outside your training."}]}, {"id": "l6-interactive-6", "title": "Map home, childcare and the journey between", "referenceSection": "l6-9", "teach": ["The whole day may cross home, childcare and transport. Ask who provides breakfast, when lunch and snacks happen, whether containers are manageable and whether something familiar is available. Descriptive information is more useful than calling a child a good or bad eater. Shared routines work best when adults avoid shame and comparison.", "Food access changes which suggestions are realistic. Ask privately whether food runs out and whether there is equipment, refrigeration, storage and time. Budget education cannot create income. Respectful support may mean connecting the family with resources or using familiar affordable foods rather than requiring speciality purchases. Keep movement and play separate from earning or compensating for food."], "type": "choice", "prompt": "A family reports that the preschool lunchbox comes home full. Which first step gives you the most useful information?", "options": [{"label": "Tell the family to buy expensive specialist snacks immediately.", "feedback": "This assumes the problem and may add cost without improving access or participation.", "correct": false}, {"label": "Ask staff to withhold play until the lunch is finished.", "feedback": "This uses pressure and treats play as a reward for intake rather than addressing the feeding environment.", "correct": false}, {"label": "Ask about the setting, timing, container access, familiar foods and the child’s experience before changing the menu.", "feedback": "Correct. These details may explain the barrier and guide a realistic plan.", "correct": true}]}], "practice": {"takeaway": "What this means for my practice: support the feeding relationship, interpret numbers carefully and recognize when a child needs coordinated clinical care.", "ask": ["What has changed in appetite, growth, stools, comfort or feeding skills?", "Which foods and drinks are reliably accepted, and how much is usually offered or consumed?", "What does the child find predictable or difficult about textures, flavours, timing and the eating environment?", "Which supplements, allergies, diagnoses and written feeding plans are already in place?", "What is the family’s most important concern, and what is realistic within their time, budget and culture?"], "try": ["Offer a small starting portion with more available, rather than a maximum the child must obey.", "Practise explaining the difference between an RDA, a historical shortcut and an individualized prescription.", "Help the family identify one safe food variation or routine change while retaining accepted foods.", "Review actual drink quantities and product labels without equating fortification with a complete diet.", "Document strengths, concerns, uncertainty and an agreed follow-up instead of using blame or a weight label as the plan."], "refer": ["Seek prompt medical and feeding/swallowing assessment for coughing with feeds, prolonged exhausting meals, pain, weight loss or a persistently narrowing food repertoire.", "Use emergency care for acute breathing difficulty, severe allergic symptoms or a seriously unwell child.", "Coordinate suspected celiac disease, deficiency, disability-related feeding needs, transfusion-related iron questions and therapeutic diets with the responsible clinical team."], "reflection": "How would you respond to a caregiver who says, “Juice is the only thing I know my child will take,” while protecting both nutrition and the caregiver’s trust?"}, "sources": [{"label": "Health Canada: macronutrient reference values", "url": "https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/food-nutrition/healthy-eating/dietary-reference-intakes/tables/reference-values-macronutrients.html"}, {"label": "Health Canada: mineral reference values", "url": "https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/food-nutrition/healthy-eating/dietary-reference-intakes/tables/reference-values-elements.html"}, {"label": "CPS: picky eating", "url": "https://caringforkids.cps.ca/handouts/healthy-living/when_your_child_is_a_picky_eater"}, {"label": "CDC: repeated food opportunities", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/infant-toddler-nutrition/foods-and-drinks/picky-eaters.html"}, {"label": "CPS: snacks and juice", "url": "https://caringforkids.cps.ca/handouts/healthy-living/healthy_snacks_for_children"}, {"label": "HealthLink BC: vegetarian feeding", "url": "https://www.healthlinkbc.ca/healthlinkbc-files/vegetarian-feeding-guidelines-babies-and-toddlers"}, {"label": "CDC: child BMI categories", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/child-teen-calculator/bmi-categories.html"}, {"label": "CDC: BMI screening context", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/obesity/child-obesity-screening/index.html"}, {"label": "HealthLink BC: choking prevention", "url": "https://www.healthlinkbc.ca/healthlinkbc-files/preventing-choking-babies-and-young-children-child-care-providers"}, {"label": "CPS: autism follow-up and feeding", "url": "https://cps.ca/en/documents/position/asd-post-diagnostic-management"}, {"label": "CPS: cerebral palsy care", "url": "https://cps.ca/en/documents/position/care-for-children-and-youth-with-cerebral-palsy"}, {"label": "NICHD: Rett syndrome causes", "url": "https://www.nichd.nih.gov/health/topics/rett/conditioninfo/causes"}, {"label": "NIDDK: celiac diagnosis", "url": "https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/digestive-diseases/celiac-disease/diagnosis"}, {"label": "CDC: sickle-cell complications and transfusion iron", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/sickle-cell/about/prevention-and-treatment.html"}, {"label": "CPS: food allergy and severe reactions", "url": "https://caringforkids.cps.ca/handouts/healthy-living/food_allergies_and_intolerances"}]};
window.NC_LEARNING_BLUEPRINTS["lesson-6"] = {"duration": "65–85 minutes, or one module at a time", "level": "Nutrition counsellor in training", "why": "Support young children and caregivers with substantial teaching, practical decisions and clear clinical boundaries rather than rigid feeding rules.", "outcomes": ["Explain changing appetite and apply responsive feeding without pressure.", "Calculate and interpret nutrient and growth numbers while stating their limitations.", "Investigate food and drink patterns, safe preparation and feeding concerns.", "Distinguish individual disability and medical needs from stereotypes or blanket restrictions.", "Finish with a feasible family action, a referral decision and a plan to review progress."], "order": ["l6-interactive-1", "l6-interactive-2", "l6-interactive-7", "l6-interactive-9", "l6-interactive-6", "l6-interactive-10", "l6-interactive-11", "l6-interactive-4", "l6-interactive-12", "l6-interactive-3", "l6-interactive-13", "l6-interactive-5", "l6-interactive-8", "l6-interactive-14", "l6-interactive-15", "l6-interactive-16", "l6-interactive-17", "l6-interactive-18", "l6-interactive-19", "l6-review-test-map", "l6-review-practice", "l6-review-transfer"], "modules": [{"id": "relationship", "label": "Module 1", "title": "Feed a growing person, not a number", "stepIds": ["l6-interactive-1", "l6-interactive-2", "l6-interactive-7", "l6-interactive-9", "l6-interactive-6"], "summary": "Understand appetite, structure, learning and growth interpretation.", "highlight": "A feeding relationship needs both structure and responsiveness."}, {"id": "nutrients", "label": "Module 2", "title": "Use nutrient numbers with their assumptions", "stepIds": ["l6-interactive-10", "l6-interactive-11", "l6-interactive-4", "l6-interactive-12"], "summary": "Practise fat arithmetic, distinguish fibre frameworks and plan nutrient sources.", "highlight": "Reference values guide reasoning; they are not automatic prescriptions."}, {"id": "everyday", "label": "Module 3", "title": "Make everyday feeding safer and more workable", "stepIds": ["l6-interactive-3", "l6-interactive-13", "l6-interactive-5", "l6-interactive-8"], "summary": "Investigate the juice case, expand safely and recognize referral signs.", "highlight": "Protect reliable intake while addressing the most important concern."}, {"id": "complexity", "label": "Module 4", "title": "Individualize support and know your boundaries", "stepIds": ["l6-interactive-14", "l6-interactive-15", "l6-interactive-16", "l6-interactive-17", "l6-interactive-18", "l6-interactive-19", "l6-review-test-map", "l6-review-practice", "l6-review-transfer"], "summary": "Work through disability, sensory needs, celiac disease, transfusions and allergy emergencies.", "highlight": "A diagnosis does not replace an individual feeding assessment."}], "stepHighlights": {}, "recap": ["Growth and appetite vary; assess the pattern rather than one meal.", "Small initial portions are invitations, not limits; repeated exposure does not require forced tasting.", "Keep current nutrient references separate from historical shortcuts and supplement doses.", "A fortified drink cannot substitute for assessment of the whole eating pattern.", "Respect sensory needs and family priorities while acting on swallowing, growth, allergy and medical concerns."], "application": {"title": "A narrow diet needs a thoughtful next step", "scenario": "A caregiver of a 3-year-old reports a short list of accepted foods and 40 fluid ounces of fortified juice most days. They are worried about nutrients but afraid the child will eat nothing if familiar foods are removed.", "stages": [{"title": "1 · Clarify before changing", "prompt": "What do you need to understand first?", "points": ["Check growth follow-up, pain, stools, swallowing and any acute symptoms.", "Confirm drink quantities, usual foods and whether the record is typical.", "Ask what the caregiver believes the juice provides and what changes feel possible."]}, {"title": "2 · Avoid the wrong inference", "prompt": "What does this record not prove?", "points": ["Bland taste does not itself diagnose a feeding disorder.", "Fortification does not demonstrate adequate energy, protein or every nutrient.", "A small repertoire is not evidence that the child or caregiver is deliberately difficult."]}, {"title": "3 · Make one safe plan", "prompt": "What can you help with now?", "points": ["Preserve accepted foods while discussing one appropriate variation and the drink routine.", "Arrange medical or feeding-team input when nutritional, growth, pain or swallowing concerns are present.", "Agree what will be observed and how to review the plan without pressure or blame."]}]}, "integratedSteps": [{"id": "l6-interactive-1", "title": "A smaller appetite is not automatically a problem", "activityId": "l6-interactive-1", "sectionIds": ["l6-1", "l6-2"], "keyIdea": "After infancy, growth generally slows and appetite may become less predictable."}, {"id": "l6-interactive-2", "title": "Offer structure without taking over the child’s appetite", "activityId": "l6-interactive-2", "sectionIds": ["l6-3"], "keyIdea": "Caregivers can organize what food is offered, where eating happens and regular opportunities for meals and snacks."}, {"id": "l6-interactive-7", "title": "Exposure is an opportunity, not a countdown", "activityId": "l6-interactive-7", "sectionIds": ["l6-4"], "keyIdea": "An unfamiliar food may need many calm encounters."}, {"id": "l6-interactive-9", "title": "Calculate BMI, then pause before interpreting", "activityId": "l6-interactive-9", "sectionIds": ["l6-8"], "keyIdea": "BMI divides weight in kilograms by height in metres squared."}, {"id": "l6-interactive-6", "title": "Map home, childcare and the journey between", "activityId": "l6-interactive-6", "sectionIds": [], "keyIdea": "Look across the whole day and the child’s actual access to food before changing the menu."}, {"id": "l6-interactive-10", "title": "Turn a fat percentage into grams", "activityId": "l6-interactive-10", "sectionIds": ["l6-5"], "keyIdea": "The fat AMDR is 30–40% of energy for ages 1–3 and 25–35% for ages 4–18."}, {"id": "l6-interactive-11", "title": "Keep a fibre shortcut separate from the DRI", "activityId": "l6-interactive-11", "sectionIds": ["l6-7"], "keyIdea": "The older “age plus five grams” shortcut gives 10 g for a 5-year-old."}, {"id": "l6-interactive-4", "title": "A nutrient reference is not a supplement prescription", "activityId": "l6-interactive-4", "sectionIds": ["l6-6"], "keyIdea": "Iron supports oxygen transport and development."}, {"id": "l6-interactive-12", "title": "Plan a plant-based pattern, not just a protein total", "activityId": "l6-interactive-12", "sectionIds": ["l6-10"], "keyIdea": "A vegetarian or vegan eating pattern needs deliberate planning for a young child’s small appetite and growth."}, {"id": "l6-interactive-3", "title": "Add the drinks before changing the menu", "activityId": "l6-interactive-3", "sectionIds": ["l6-12"], "keyIdea": "The supplied 3-year-old case lists juice at breakfast, lunch, snack and dinner: 8 + 8 + 12 + 12 fluid ounces."}, {"id": "l6-interactive-13", "title": "Build from an accepted food without hiding the concern", "activityId": "l6-interactive-13", "sectionIds": [], "keyIdea": "The case includes oatmeal, banana, buttered macaroni, crackers and white bread."}, {"id": "l6-interactive-5", "title": "Nutrients do not cancel choking risk", "activityId": "l6-interactive-5", "sectionIds": ["l6-11"], "keyIdea": "A food may contain useful nutrients but still be unsafe in its current form."}, {"id": "l6-interactive-8", "title": "Know when “picky” is not enough of an explanation", "activityId": "l6-interactive-8", "sectionIds": ["l6-13"], "keyIdea": "Food selectivity exists on a spectrum."}, {"id": "l6-interactive-14", "title": "Do not let a diagnosis become an energy formula", "activityId": "l6-interactive-14", "sectionIds": ["l6-14"], "keyIdea": "Children with cerebral palsy or Down syndrome may have feeding, growth and health needs that differ from those of peers."}, {"id": "l6-interactive-15", "title": "Describe sensory needs, not stereotypes", "activityId": "l6-interactive-15", "sectionIds": ["l6-15"], "keyIdea": "Autistic children are individuals."}, {"id": "l6-interactive-16", "title": "Protect the diagnostic pathway before removing gluten", "activityId": "l6-interactive-16", "sectionIds": ["l6-16"], "keyIdea": "Celiac disease is an immune-mediated condition triggered by gluten, not simply a preference for avoiding wheat."}, {"id": "l6-interactive-17", "title": "Transfusion-related iron is not a food-ranking exercise", "activityId": "l6-interactive-17", "sectionIds": ["l6-17"], "keyIdea": "Sickle-cell disease and iron-deficiency anemia are different conditions."}, {"id": "l6-interactive-18", "title": "Separate allergy emergencies from routine asthma counselling", "activityId": "l6-interactive-18", "sectionIds": [], "keyIdea": "An allergic reaction to food can include breathing difficulty or wheeze, sometimes as part of anaphylaxis."}, {"id": "l6-interactive-19", "title": "Turn the whole story into one feasible next step", "activityId": "l6-interactive-19", "sectionIds": ["l6-9", "l6-18"], "keyIdea": "A food record is a starting point, not a verdict."}, {"id": "l6-review-test-map", "title": "Prepare for the test without memorizing an error", "sectionIds": ["l6-19"], "prompt": "Name two places where the source test requires a historical assumption or additional context. What would you say instead in practice?", "keyIdea": "Examples include age-plus-five fibre versus the current AI, rounded BMI calculations versus precise chart interpretation, and disability or food-allergy generalizations. Explain the concept and preserve uncertainty rather than inventing an official answer key."}, {"id": "l6-review-practice", "title": "Retrieve, calculate and explain your reasoning", "sectionIds": ["l6-20"], "prompt": "Choose one calculation and one case from the practice set. Explain the steps and why a tempting alternative would be wrong before checking the explanation.", "keyIdea": "A strong answer includes the relevant units or assumption, the reasoning that links evidence to the conclusion, and the limits of what a short case can establish."}, {"id": "l6-review-transfer", "title": "Turn knowledge into a safe consultation", "sectionIds": ["l6-21"], "prompt": "What would you ask, try and refer in a consultation about restricted variety and high juice intake?", "keyIdea": "Ask about safety, growth, usual intake, skills and family priorities; agree on one feasible change that preserves reliable intake; refer growth, swallowing, medical or significant nutritional concerns rather than managing them independently."}]};
